    "ref_doc": "GRI Standards 2021.pdf",
    "source_text": "Disclosure 305-6 Emissions of ozone-depleting substances (ODS) The reporting organization shall report the following information: Compilation requirements Production of ODS = - -ODS produced ODS destroyed by approved technologies ODS entirely used as feedstock in the manufacture of other chemicals 2.11.2 exclude ODS recycled and reused.REQUIREMENTS Production, imports, and exports of ODS in metric tons of CFC-11 (trichlorofluoromethane) equivalent .a. Substances included in the calculation. b. Source of the emission factors used. c. Standards, methodologies, assumptions, and/or calculation tools used. d. When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 305-6, the reporting organization shall:2.11 calculate the production of ODS as the amount of ODS produced, minus the amount destroyed by approved technologies, and minus the amount entirely used as feedstock in the manufacture of other chemicals;2.11.1 2.12 When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 305-6, the reporting organization should: 2.12.1 if subject to different standards and methodologies, describe the approach to selecting them; 2.12.2 where it aids transparency or comparability over time, provide a breakdown of the ODS data by: 2.12.2.1 business unit or facility; 2.12.2.2 country; 2.12.2.3 type of source; 2.12.2.4 type of activity.RECOMMENDATIONS Guidance for Disclosure 305-6 The reporting organization can report separate or combined data for the substances included in the calculation. Background Measuring ODS production, imports, and exports helps to indicate how an organization complies with legislation. This is particularly relevant if the organization produces or uses ODS in its processes, products and services and is subject to phase-out commitments. Results on ODS phase-out help to indicate the organization’s position in any markets affected by regulation on ODS. This disclosure covers the substances included in Annexes A, B, C, and E of the ‘Montreal Protocol’ as well as any other ODS produced, imported, or exported by an organization. See references [1], [2], [8] and [9] in the Bibliography .GUIDANCE GRI 305: Emissions 2016 390\n\n[Page 392]\nDisclosure 305-7 Nitrogen oxides (NOx), sulfur oxides (SOx), and other significant air emissions The reporting organization shall report the following information: Compilation requirementsREQUIREMENTS Significant air emissions , in kilograms or multiples, for each of the following: NO x i. SO x ii. Persistent organic pollutants (POP) iii. Volatile organic compounds (VOC) iv. Hazardous air pollutants (HAP) v. Particulate matter (PM) vi. Other standard categories of air emissions identified in relevant regulations vii.a. Source of the emission factors used. b. Standards, methodologies, assumptions, and/or calculation tools used. c. When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 305-7, the reporting organization shall select one of the following approaches for calculating significant air emissions:2.13 Direct measurement of emissions (such as online analyzers); 2.13.1 Calculation based on site-specific data; 2.13.2 Calculation based on published emission factors; 2.13.3 Estimation. If estimations are used due to a lack of default figures, the organization shall indicate the basis on which figures were estimated.2.13.4 RECOMMENDATIONSWhen compiling the information specified in Disclosure 305-7, the reporting organization should:2.14 if subject to different standards and methodologies, describe the approach to selecting them;2.14.1 where it aids transparency or comparability over time, provide a breakdown of the air emissions data by:2.14.2 business unit or facility; 2.14.2.1 country; 2.14.2.2 type of source; 2.14.2.3 type of activity. 2.14.2.4 See references [3], [4], [5], [6] and [10] in the Bibliography .GUIDANCE GRI 305: Emissions 2016 391",
    "new_id": 26,
    "attempt": "A",
    "reasoning": "\nThe objective is to identify the specific condition that makes emissions data for ozone-depleting substances non-compliant with the 2021 GRI Consolidated Set. I need to clarify what non-compliance means here. It could mean a failure to meet environmental thresholds, but in the context of ESG reporting standards, it more likely refers to a failure to meet the specific disclosure and calculation requirements of the GRI framework. The term ozone-depleting substances is the subject, and the actors are the reporting entity and its data providers. The mechanism involves the calculation process, the inputs, and the disclosure of those inputs. I must distinguish between the substance's role in a process and the mathematical treatment of the data.\n\nLet's start with a hypothesis centered on the accuracy of the chemical data itself. Perhaps the data is non-compliant if the wrong substances are included. For instance, if a company reports emissions of greenhouse gases instead of ODS, that would be a clear error. However, the question asks for a condition related to the requirements of the GRI Standards 2021, not a simple mistake in chemical identity. The GRI standards are more concerned with the transparency of the calculation and the inclusion of specific categories of activity than with the chemical taxonomy itself.\n\nLet's pivot to the mechanism of estimation. If a company cannot obtain direct emission factors, they must estimate. This is a common occurrence in environmental reporting. If they use an estimation method because default figures are unavailable, does that violate the standards? The GRI often requires disclosure of estimation methods to ensure transparency. If the company uses an estimate but fails to disclose the basis of that estimate, they are violating the transparency requirement. This would make the data non-compliant. This is a strong candidate. The logic is: Estimation is allowed, but lack of disclosure of the estimation basis is not.\n\nNow, let's consider the inclusion of recycled substances. If a company includes ODS that have been recycled and reused in their total emissions calculation, is that non-compliant? One might think that recycling is a mitigation activity, and including it would inflate the total. However, in ESG reporting, the goal is often to provide a complete picture of the lifecycle and the actual environmental impact. If the standard requires a total emissions figure, omitting the recycled portion might actually be the error. If the standard specifically allows for the inclusion of recycled and reused substances to reflect the actual net impact, then excluding them would be the non-compliance. But if the standard mandates that recycled ODS must be included to avoid understating the total, then the inclusion is actually the correct procedure. This path is confusing because it depends on whether the standard treats recycling as a subtraction or as a part of the total mass flow. I need to look closer at the wording of the GRI requirements regarding mass balance.\n\nLet's try a third path: the feedstock exclusion. The question asks about a condition that would make the reported data non-compliant. If a company excludes ODS that are entirely used as feedstock in the manufacture of other chemicals, is that a violation? I need to define feedstock versus direct emission. If ODS are used to create a new chemical, the ODS themselves are not being emitted into the atmosphere; they are being transformed. If the standard only requires reporting on direct emissions or releases, then excluding the feedstock might be the correct procedure. However, if the GRI requires a comprehensive report of all ODS managed by the company, regardless of their fate, then excluding the feedstock would be a gap in the data, making the report non-compliant. This feels like a plausible direction, but I must be careful. Is the requirement about the amount of ODS used, or only the amount released?\n\nWait, I should reconsider the estimation path. I previously thought that using an estimate was fine as long as it was disclosed. But what if the issue is the lack of default figures itself? If the company cannot provide a figure because no default is available, does that make the data non-compliant? Not necessarily, as long as they provide an estimate and disclose the method. The problem specifically mentions the lack of default figures as a condition for the estimation. This suggests the non-compliance is tied to the failure to provide a value when a default is required or when the specific conditions for estimation are not met. If the standard requires a figure and none is provided because of a lack of data, and that gap is not addressed through estimation and disclosure, the data is incomplete.\n\nLet's test the combined data hypothesis. If a company reports a single number for all substances listed in Annexes A, B, C, and E of the Montreal Protocol, is that non-compliant? The Montreal Protocol annexes categorize different types of ozone-depleting substances. If the GRI requires separate reporting for different classes of substances to ensure regulatory alignment, then combining them would be a violation of the granularity required by the standard. This is a distinct possibility. The logic is: If the standard requires separate reporting for different annexes to match international regulations, then a lump-sum report fails to provide the necessary detail for stakeholders to assess risk.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the feedstock idea. If I am a chemical manufacturer, I use ODS to create a new product. That ODS is not emitted. If I report zero emissions, am I compliant? If the standard only cares about the release to the atmosphere, I am compliant. But if the standard cares about the total ODS managed, I am not. However, the question asks about emissions of ODS. In environmental accounting, emissions usually refer to the release into the environment. If the ODS are not released, they are not emissions. But if the standard defines emissions as the total quantity of ODS handled that results in depletion, the exclusion of feedstock might be the error. \n\nLet's look for a counterexample. Suppose a company reports zero emissions and uses no estimation. They are very precise. Are they compliant? Yes. Now, suppose they use an estimate but disclose the method. Are they compliant? Yes. Now, suppose they include recycled ODS to show their net impact. Is that compliant? It might be, if the standard allows it. Now, suppose they exclude the feedstock. If the standard only cares about releases, they are still compliant. This suggests that the feedstock exclusion might not be the primary driver of non-compliance unless the standard specifically mandates the inclusion of all ODS managed.\n\nLet's go back to the estimation issue. The problem mentions the lack of default figures as a trigger for estimation. If the company cannot provide a figure because of this lack, and they do not provide an estimate, they are missing data. If the GRI requires that all reportable data be provided or a reasonable estimate be used, then the lack of a figure due to a lack of default data, without an estimation workaround, is a direct failure of the reporting requirement. This seems like the most robust path. It distinguishes between the inability to provide a figure and the failure to fulfill the reporting obligation through estimation.\n\nLet's test the combined data path again. If the standard says report Annex A and Annex B separately, and the company says \"we used 100 units of all ODS,\" they have failed the requirement for granularity. This is a clear-cut violation of the procedure. It is different from the estimation issue, which is about the uncertainty of a value, and the feedstock issue, which is about the definition of the boundary of what constitutes an emission. The combined data issue is about the structure and categorization of the report.\n\nI need to weigh the estimation-based non-compliance against the combined-data non-compliance. The estimation path relies on the interaction between the lack of default figures and the requirement to disclose the basis of the estimate. The combined data path relies on the requirement for specific categorization. Both are valid under different interpretations of \"non-compliance.\" However, the estimation path is more fundamental to the GRI's focus on transparency and the mitigation of information asymmetry. If a company reports a number without a basis, the stakeholder cannot assess the risk. If a company combines categories, the stakeholder cannot assess the specific risk associated with a particular class of substance. Both are significant.\n\nIs there a conflict between the two? If a company combines data to simplify reporting, they might think they are being efficient. But if the GRI requires separation, they are non-compliant. If they use an estimate, they are being transparent, but if they don't disclose the basis, they are non-compliant. The strongest direction must identify the specific condition that violates the 2021 GRI Consolidated Set requirements. The condition involving the lack of default figures and the requirement for estimation disclosure is a procedural failure that directly impacts the reliability of the data. The condition involving the combination of different annexes is a structural failure that impacts the comparability of the data. I must determine which of these is the most direct violation of the specific requirements mentioned. The estimation issue is a failure of the disclosure mechanism, whereas the combined data issue is a failure of the classification mechanism. Both are distinct types of non-compliance. I need to focus on which one is being targeted by the specific wording of the question regarding the Consolidated Set. The question asks for the condition that would render the reported data non-compliant. The lack of default figures and the failure to use estimation when necessary is a primary reason why data would fail to meet the requirements for transparency and completeness.\n**Final Answer**"
