    "ref_doc": "GRI Standards 2021.pdf",
    "source_text": "Disclosure 303-2 Management of water discharge- related impacts The reporting organization shall report the following information:REQUIREMENTS A description of any minimum standards set for the quality of effluent discharge, and how these minimum standards were determined, including: how standards for facilities operating in locations with no local discharge requirements were determined;i. any internally developed water quality standards or guidelines; ii. any sector-specific standards considered; iii. whether the profile of the receiving waterbody was considered. iv.a. Guidance for Disclosure 303-2 Minimum standards are those that go beyond regulatory requirements in controlling the quality of effluent discharge. Water quality refers to the physical, chemical, biological, and taste-related characteristics of water. It is a measure of water suitability for a given purpose or function, including its use as a human right . Water quality standards help uphold water quality in order to protect ecosystems, wildlife, and human health and welfare, and can be based on water properties, such as temperature or pH value. The specific choice of water quality standards and parameters can vary depending on an organization’s procucts, services, and facility locations, and can depend on national and/or regional regulations, as well as the profile of the receiving waterbody.GUIDANCE GRI 303: Water and Effluents 2018 342\n\n[Page 344]\n2. Topic disclosures Disclosure 303-3 Water withdrawal The reporting organization shall report the following information: Compilation requirementsREQUIREMENTS Total water withdrawal from all areas in megaliters, and a breakdown of this total by the following sources, if applicable: Surface water ; i. Groundwater ; ii. Seawater ; iii. Produced water ; iv. Third-party water . v.a. Total water withdrawal from all areas with water stress in megaliters, and a breakdown of this total by the following sources, if applicable: Surface water; i. Groundwater; ii. Seawater; iii. Produced water; iv. Third-party water, and a breakdown of this total by the withdrawal sources listed in i-iv.v.b. A breakdown of total water withdrawal from each of the sources listed in Disclosures 303-3-a and 303-3-b in megaliters by the following categories: Freshwater (≤1,000 mg/L Total Dissolved Solids); i. Other water (>1,000 mg/L Total Dissolved Solids). ii.c. Any contextual information necessary to understand how the data have been compiled, such as any standards, methodologies, and assumptions used.d. When compiling the information specified in Disclosure 303-3, the reporting organization shall use publicly available and credible tools and methodologies for assessing water stress in an area.2.1 RECOMMENDATIONSThe reporting organization should report the following additional information: 2.2 A breakdown of total water withdrawal in megaliters by withdrawal source categories listed in Disclosure 303-3, at each facility in areas with water stress;2.2.1 Total water withdrawal in megaliters by suppliers with significant water-related impacts in areas with water stress.2.2.2 Background The volume of water withdrawal from areas with water stress can indicate an organization’s impacts in sensitive locations. To learn more about locations where water-related impacts might be significant, and where actions to address them are most needed, the reporting organization can also report the information requested in Disclosure 303-3 for each facility in areas with water stress. This can give stakeholders more confidence in the organization’s water stewardship efforts and practices. Guidance for Disclosure 303-3 For an example of how to present information on requirements in Disclosure 303-3, see Table 1. Surface water includes collected or harvested rainwater. Third-party water includes water supplied by municipal water networks or other organizations.GUIDANCE GRI 303: Water and Effluents 2018 343",
    "new_id": 56,
    "attempt": "D",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in understanding the motivation for a voluntary action in an environment where the primary driver, regulatory compliance, is absent. I must first clarify the term internal water quality standards. This refers to benchmarks set by the organization itself, rather than those mandated by external bodies. The term discharge requirements refers to the specific thresholds for wastewater that a company is legally required to meet before releasing it back into the environment. The question asks for the best explanation within the context of the GRI Standards 2021, which emphasizes materiality and the protection of environmental and social systems. I need to determine if the organization is acting to mitigate physical risks, manage reputational risks, or satisfy stakeholder expectations.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis that the organization is acting to align with sector-specific standards. If an organization develops internal standards to match sector-specific standards, they are essentially adopting a de facto industry benchmark. However, if there are no local discharge requirements, the sector standard might be higher or lower than what the local ecosystem actually needs. If the sector standard is lower than what is necessary to protect the local watershed, the organization is not actually addressing the gap left by the absence of regulation; they are simply following a potentially inadequate industry norm. This does not explain why they would develop their own standards in a vacuum of non-existent local rules. Therefore, this path is insufficient because it fails to address the specific void created by the lack of local regulation.\n\nAnother possibility is that the organization is using internal standards as a proxy for national water stress assessment tools. This would mean the organization is using a broader, national-level metric to guide their local behavior. But an assessment tool is a different mechanism than a water quality standard. An assessment tool identifies the level of water stress, such as scarcity or competition, but it does not dictate the chemical or biological parameters of the water itself. You cannot discharge water that is safe for a high-stress area if the specific local ecosystem requires different parameters, such as a specific pH level or a certain level of dissolved oxygen. Thus, using a water stress assessment tool does not solve the problem of how to manage the actual quality of the discharge. This path is a category error, conflating the measurement of scarcity with the specification of quality.\n\nI should also consider if the organization is prioritizing withdrawal sources based on total dissolved solids to manage water stress. This is a procedure focused on the intake of water. The question, however, specifically asks about the development of water quality standards, which are concerned with the output or the discharge of water. While managing the intake is a vital part of water stewardship, it does not explain why an organization would create a standard for the quality of its discharge in the absence of local rules. If the goal were simply to manage water stress, they might focus on reducing volume or improving efficiency, but that does not necessarily require the creation of a new set of quality standards. This path is a distraction because it focuses on the wrong part of the water cycle.\n\nThis leaves me with the idea that the organization is attempting to fill a regulatory vacuum to protect ecosystem integrity. If there are no local discharge requirements, the default state is a lack of oversight. In this scenario, the organization faces a material risk: the potential for their operations to cause long-term ecological damage that could eventually lead to stricter regulations, or damage that impacts the local community's health or the availability of the resource. By developing internal standards, the organization is proactively addressing these impacts on ecosystems, wildlife, and human health. This is a mechanism of risk mitigation. The objective is to maintain the functional integrity of the local watershed even when there is no legal mandate to do so. This path is the most robust because it directly addresses the causal consequence of the absence of local regulation: the potential for unmanaged environmental impact. The organization is not just following a rule; it is attempting to prevent the very need for a rule by internalizing the externalities that local regulations would otherwise manage.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
