    "correct_answer": "B",
    "ref_page": "287-288",
    "ref_doc": "CDP 7.pdf",
    "source_text": "286 • In addition to any absolute targets, companies should disclose company-wide CO2 and/or fuel economy targets for products and, where relevant, for specific markets. Targets should be expressed in grams of CO2 per kilometer. Note for financial services sector companies: • Select “Absolute target” or “Intensity target” only if you have any climate targets covering your operational emissions, i.e. Scope 1, Scope 2 and Scope 3 emissions excluding Category 15 Investments. Select “Portfolio target” for any other climate target types related to your lending, investment and insurance portfolios. Note for capital goods sector companies: • Companies should consider reporting company-wide and/or product-level Scope 3 targets, and in particular, Scope 3 targets relating to the use of sold products. Additional information Examples of emissions reduction targets The following are examples of absolute targets: • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction from base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction in product use phase relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction in supply chain relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction relative to 5 year rolling average of emissions • Cap on emissions in metric tons CO2e The following are examples of intensity targets: • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per unit revenue (also per unit turnover; per unit gross sales) relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per full-time employee equivalent (also per hours worked; per operating hour; per guest night; per capita; per patient days) relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per unit of product (e.g. metric ton of paper; metric ton of aluminum) relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per passenger kilometer (also per km; per nautical mile) relative to base year • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per square foot relative to base year • Cap on emissions relative to an activity (e.g. stabilizing emissions at x metric tons CO2e per metric to of steel produced) • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction per MWh • Metric tons CO2e or % reduction in emissions from business flights per employee Tags Authority type All requesters Environmental Issue (Theme) Question level CC Questionnaire sector Question level All (7.53.1) Provide details of your absolute emissions targets and progress made against those targets.\n\n[Page 288]\n287 Question details Question dependencies • This question only appears if you select “Absolute target” in response to 7.53. Change from last year • Minor change Rationale Target setting plays a vital role in environmental action through its role in the successful execution of corporate strategies, as well as in the effective management of dependencies, impacts, risks, and opportunities. The question encourages organizations to set and make progress towards timebound, tracked, quantitative targets, informed by the guidance of leading initiatives and frameworks, such as the Science Based Targets initiative where available. Ambition Organizations make progress against emissions targets that reflect their full emissions inventory and are in line with the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) criteria. Response options • Please complete the following table. The table is displayed over several rows for readability. You are able to add rows by using the “Add Row” function at the bottom of the table.",
    "new_id": 194,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the distinction between different types of emissions reporting within the financial services sector, specifically as governed by the CDP Full Corporate Questionnaire April 2025. I must first clarify the ambiguous terms. An absolute target refers to a reduction in total metric tons of greenhouse gases, regardless of company growth. An intensity target, conversely, is a reduction measured relative to a business metric, such as revenue or headcount. The term Category 15 Investments refers to a specific classification in the GHG Protocol, likely relating to the acquisition and ownership of real property. A correct solution must identify the specific condition where these two target types—absolute and intensity—are the appropriate mechanisms for reporting.\n\nI will first test the hypothesis that the selection of these target types is driven by the presence of investment-related emissions. If a company has climate targets covering emissions from its investments, it might seem logical to use these targets. However, if the emissions are categorized under Category 15, they are treated as operational emissions rather than portfolio emissions. If the guidance requires distinguishing between the two, then selecting targets for Category 15 Investments would likely fall under a different reporting mechanism. Therefore, the presence of investment targets is not the primary condition for selecting absolute or intensity targets in the context of the other options provided.\n\nLet me pivot to a different path. Perhaps the condition is the exclusion of a specific type of emissions to ensure the targets are meaningful. If a company's targets pertain to operational emissions excluding Category 15 Investments, this creates a specific boundary. By removing Category 15, the company is focusing its absolute or intensity targets on its core operational footprint. This distinguishes the company's direct impact from its investment-related impact. This seems more plausible because it defines the scope of the target rather than just stating that targets exist.\n\nI must then consider if the condition relates to the nature of the emissions themselves, such as Scope 3. If the targets are for portfolio-wide Scope 3 emissions unrelated to investments, does that fit? Scope 3 typically involves value chain emissions. If the emissions are Scope 3 but not related to the company's investments, they would be operational in nature. However, the phrasing \"unrelated to investments\" is somewhat redundant if we are already talking about Scope 3. I need to see if there is a distinction between operational emissions and investment-related emissions that is more fundamental than just the Scope number.\n\nLet's look at the actors and the mechanisms. The actors are the financial services companies. The mechanism is the selection of target types. The output is the reported emission reduction. If the company is reporting on business flights per employee, is that an intensity target? Yes, because it is a reduction in emissions per employee. But does this cover the broader requirement of the question? A single metric like flights per employee is a very narrow slice of a company's footprint. The question asks for a condition under which they should select these targets, implying a broader scope.\n\nI am now reconsidering the relationship between the exclusion of Category 15 and the concept of operational emissions. If a target is to be an \"absolute target\" or an \"intensity target,\" it must be grounded in a clear operational boundary. If the guidance requires separating investment emissions (Category 15) from operational emissions, then the condition for using these specific target types is the reporting of operational emissions that have had that specific investment category removed. This creates a clear distinction between the company's physical footprint and its financial footprint. \n\nIs it possible that the condition is simply that the targets must be for operational emissions? If so, why include the mention of Category 15 in the options? The inclusion of Category 15 suggests that the distinction between operational and investment emissions is the critical variable. If I choose the option that says \"When they have climate targets covering emissions from their investments,\" I am ignoring the specific mechanism of exclusion that is present in the other options. The most robust direction must involve the tension between operational emissions and the specific exclusion of Category 15 Investments to define the scope of the targets. This allows for a clear differentiation between the company's direct operational impact and its investment-related impact, which is the primary objective of the guidance.\n**Final Answer**"
