    "id": 16701,
    "question": "When calculating total renewable energy consumption, which of the following scenarios would require splitting the energy source into renewable and non-renewable components according to the provided guidelines, as outlined in CDP Full Corporate Questionnaire April 2025 – Module 7?",
    "options": {
      "D": "Blended fuels composed partially of biomass-derived materials and partially of fossil fuel derivatives.",
      "A": "Electricity generated exclusively from solar panels installed on-site.",
      "B": "Steam produced entirely from geothermal resources without any additional inputs.",
      "C": "Waste energy recovered directly from industrial processes powered by natural gas.",
      "Z": "Not sure"
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "ref_page": "142",
    "ref_doc": "CDP 7.pdf",
    "source_text": "141 • If you do not have exact consumption data, you may alternatively estimate your company’s consumption by reviewing fuel and energy purchasing orders. Consumption of self-generated non-fuel renewable energy • If your organization produces renewable energy that is not based on fuel (such as solar, wind, hydro, geothermal, marine), then any consumption of this energy should be entered here. • Consumption of renewable fuels (such as solid and liquid biofuels and biogas) should be excluded because these are accounted for in the row “Consumption of fuel (excluding feedstock)”. • All forms of non-fuel renewable energy - electricity, heat, steam, or cooling – shall be included. Total energy consumption • The data entered in each column of this row should equal the sum of all the above rows (if the above rows have been fully disclosed for). • If you do not disclose data for specific energy carriers in the rows above, but you are able to enter the total energy consumed by your organization, then you should do so. Heating value (column 2) • This column is only applicable to the consumption of fuels because it is a measure of combustion energy. In the other rows you should select \"Unable to confirm heating value”. • Fuel energy data in HHV is typically used in the United States and Canada, whereas LHV is more commonly the unit used in other countries/areas and by international bodies. If you do not know the unit applicable to your raw data, you may wish to infer it based on the location from which the data is sourced, i.e. if the fuel related data is sourced from outside of the United States and Canada, then it is likely that LHV is applicable. MWh from renewable sources (column 3) • Waste energy should not be included if it is derived from fossil fuels. • Hydrogen should not be included if it is derived from fossil fuels. • Blended fuels deriving from both renewable and non-renewable sources should be split by the proportion contained from each source. For municipal waste and refuse-derived fuel, only the fraction of the fuel that is derived from biomass can be included as renewable energy, when calculating renewable energy consumption totals. Further explanations of municipal waste and a glossary of fuel definitions is provided in the CDP Technical Note: “Fuel Definitions”. MWh from non-renewable sources (column 4) • All energy not identified as deriving from renewable sources should be entered, e.g. coal, oil, natural gas, etc. • Direct consumption of nuclear fuel should not be included, as this is covered in more detail in questions for electric utilities. Consumption of purchased or acquired electricity, steam, heat and/or cooling from nuclear sources, however, should be included.",
    "new_id": 212,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe objective is to determine which scenario necessitates the separation of renewable and non-renewable components in the calculation of total renewable energy consumption. I must first clarify the term total renewable energy consumption. In the context of ESG reporting, this typically refers to the proportion of a company's total energy intake that is derived from renewable sources. The core of the problem lies in the phrase splitting the energy source. This implies that the energy being considered is not a single, pure stream but a mixture. If the energy is a mixture, the reporting standard requires the user to quantify the renewable and non-renewable parts separately before summing them into the total renewable percentage. I need to identify which option describes a mixture and which describe pure, single-source energy.\n\nLet's examine Option A: Electricity generated exclusively from solar panels installed on-site. This is a clear, single-source input. There is no mention of any other fuel or energy source contributing to this electricity. Therefore, there is no need to split this into components. It is a 100% renewable input. This does not meet the criteria of requiring a split.\n\nMoving to Option B: Steam produced entirely from geothermal resources without any additional inputs. This is also a single-source input. The descriptor without any additional inputs is crucial. It confirms that the steam is not a blend. Since it is 100% geothermal, there is no non-renewable component to separate. This scenario also does not require a split.\n\nNow consider Option C: Waste energy recovered directly from industrial processes powered by natural gas. This is an interesting case. Is the waste energy itself a mixture? The energy is being recovered from industrial processes that are powered by natural gas. The natural gas is the primary source of the heat or energy in the industrial process. When the waste energy is recovered, it is essentially the leftover thermal energy from the combustion of natural gas. However, the question asks about the energy source that would require splitting. If I am calculating the renewable energy consumption and I include this recovered energy, I am including energy that is fundamentally tied to a non-renewable process. But does the recovered energy itself have a renewable and non-renewable component? The energy is waste energy, a single stream. The connection to the natural gas is the source of the non-renewability, but the energy being measured is the recovered stream. If the guidelines require splitting based on the source, the source here is the industrial process. Since the process is powered by natural gas, the energy recovered from it is inherently linked to a non-renewable source. This suggests a need to account for the non-renewable component of the process from which the energy was recovered. However, I must be careful. Is the waste energy itself a blend? No. It is a single stream. The complexity arises from the fact that the energy's origin is non-renewable. If the guidelines mandate splitting based on the source of the energy's generation or the process from which it was derived, then this scenario requires a split to ensure the non-renewable contribution from the natural gas is not overlooked.\n\nLet's test Option D: Blended fuels composed partially of biomass-derived materials and partially of fossil fuel derivatives. This is a clear-cut mixture. The fuel is explicitly described as blended. It contains two distinct chemical components: biomass (renewable) and fossil fuels (non-renewable). By definition, a blended fuel requires the user to determine the mass or volume fraction of each component to calculate the total renewable content accurately. This is the quintessential example of a split.\n\nI should reconsider if Option C is actually a mixture. If I define the source as the fuel used in the industrial process, then the source is a mixture of renewable and non-renewable. But the energy being consumed is the waste heat. If the waste energy is treated as a separate input, it is a single stream. If the guidelines require splitting the source, and the source is the industrial process, then the presence of natural gas in the process necessitates a split. But wait, if the waste energy is the only thing being consumed, is it a mixture? No. It is a single stream of energy. The confusion might stem from whether the \"source\" refers to the energy stream or the process that created it. If the rule is \"split the source,\" and the source is the industrial process, then the natural gas is the non-renewable part of that source. \n\nLet's look at the mechanism of the split. In Option D, the split is done at the fuel stage. In Option C, the split is done at the process stage. If the guidelines apply to any energy derived from a process that includes non-renewable inputs, then Option C requires a split. If the guidelines only apply to physical mixtures of fuels, then Option C is exempt. However, the phrase \"splitting the energy source\" is somewhat ambiguous. If I am an auditor, I want to ensure that any energy claimed as renewable is not \"tainted\" by non-renewable sources. In Option C, the energy is \"tainted\" by the natural gas used in the original industrial process. To be precise, the energy source (the industrial process) is a mixture of renewable (the waste energy's potential) and non-renewable (the natural gas). \n\nLet's test a counterexample. Suppose a company uses a furnace powered by 100% coal to create steam. They then recover waste heat from that steam. If they report the waste heat as renewable, they are misrepresenting the data. The source of the energy is the coal-fired furnace. The waste heat is a product of that non-renewable source. Therefore, to be accurate, the reporting must account for the non-renewable nature of the source. This strengthens the argument that Option C requires a split because the source (the industrial process) is a mixture of renewable and non-renewable inputs.\n\nI must distinguish between the energy stream and the energy source. The energy stream in Option C is the waste energy. The source is the industrial process. If the process is powered by natural gas, the source is a non-renewable-dominated process. If the guidelines require splitting the source, then the non-renewable contribution from the natural gas must be factored into the calculation of the total renewable energy consumption. This is a different mechanism of splitting than the physical mixture in Option D, but it is a split nonetheless. \n\nIs there any other way to interpret the guidelines? If the guidelines only required splitting for physical fuel blends, then Option C would be a false positive. But the problem asks which scenario would require splitting the energy source. If the source is defined by its inputs, and the inputs are mixed, a split is required. In Option C, the input to the industrial process is a mixture of renewable (the waste) and non-renewable (the natural gas). This is a highly complex interaction. I need to decide if the \"source\" refers to the fuel or the process. If the source is the process, then the presence of natural gas means the source is a mixed source. This requires a split to ensure the non-renewable contribution is not ignored. The most rigorous approach to ESG reporting is to trace the energy back to its most fundamental origin. If that origin includes non-renewable inputs, the reporting standard usually demands that those inputs be accounted for to prevent greenwashing. Therefore, the scenario where the energy is derived from a process that itself relies on non-renewable fuel is the most likely candidate for a required split, even if the energy being consumed is a single, recovered stream.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
