    "correct_answer": "D",
    "ref_page": "68",
    "ref_doc": "CDP 8-13.pdf",
    "source_text": "67 columns 1 “% […] traceable to production unit” to 5 “% […] from unknown origin” should account for 100% of the sourced volume you reported in 8.2, for each commodity. • If you have included embedded soy in your sourced volume, as reported in 8.2 and 8.2.1, you should include your embedded soy volume in your response to this question. • Note: when disclosing the traceability of embedded soy, report the level of traceability back to the soy production origin. If you can only trace to the origin of the animal that was fed the soy or to the soy feed supplier origin, report this in column “% of sourced volume traceable to other point…”. If you have not traced your embedded soy at all, disclose this in column “% of sourced volume from unknown origin”. % of sourced volume traceable to production unit (column 1) • If you have traced your sourced volumes to the specific production unit of origin (e.g. farm, breeding farm, plantation, ranch, or forest management unit), report the related percentage here. % of sourced volume traceable to sourcing area and not to production unit (column 2) • If you have traced your sourced volumes to an area or region where the material was produced or extracted (e.g., a radius or supply shed around a mill, processing facility or a first point of collection), but the specific production unit of origin is not known, report the related percentage here. % of sourced volume traceable to country/area of origin and not to sourcing area or production unit (column 3) • If you have not traced your sourced volume to the production unit or sourcing area where the material was produced and have only traced to the level of the country/area of origin, report the related percentage here. For further guidance on country/area lists, refer to the CDP technical note: Countries, Areas and Regions. % of sourced volume traceable to other point (i.e., processing facility/first importer) not in the country/area of origin (column 4) • If you can trace the commodity to a processing or storage facility/first importer in a different country to the country/area of origin, report the related percentage here. % of sourced volume reported [auto calculated] (column 6) • This column will be auto-calculated. The percentage of sourced volume reported will be calculated as the sum of column 1 “% […] traceable to production unit”, column 2 “% […] traceable to sourcing area and not to production unit, column 3 “% […] traceable to country/area of origin and not to sourcing area or production unit”, column 4 “% […] traceable to other point (i.e., processing facility/first importer) not in the country/area of origin” and column 5 “% […] from unknown origin”. Ensure that you have entered data into all these columns. If none of your volumes are traceable to a particular point, enter 0 (zero). These columns should be completed for all commodity rows. • Please ensure that this column adds up to 100%. Explanation of terms • Production unit: a plantation, farm, ranch, or forest management unit. This includes all plots used for agriculture or forestry that are under one management, located in the same general area, and share the same means of production. It also includes natural ecosystems, infrastructure, and other land within or associated with the plantation, farm, ranch, or forest management unit.",
    "new_id": 268,
    "attempt": "D",
    "reasoning": "\nThe objective is to identify the scenario that maximizes the percentage of sourced volume reported as unknown origin while remaining compliant with the CDP Full Corporate Questionnaire guidelines for 2025. I must first clarify what constitutes unknown origin versus known origin in this context. If the guidelines define known origin as requiring specific geographic coordinates, farm names, and production unit identifiers, then any lack of these specific data points could be interpreted as unknown. However, if the guidelines only require the country of origin to be known, then a lack of regional data does not equate to an unknown origin. I need to distinguish between the level of traceability required for compliance and the granularity of the data provided. Let us test Option A. If a company traces all volumes to specific farms but lacks data on the sourcing area, the origin is technically known because the farm is a specific location. Even if the region is unknown, the farm's existence is a known data point. Reporting this as unknown would likely be a violation of the guidelines' truth-in-reporting standards, as the company has identified the producer. Therefore, Option A is likely a low-percentage candidate for the unknown column. Moving to Option B, the company reports traceability to the country of origin but lacks regional or production unit data. If the guidelines mandate reporting the percentage of volume unknown by origin, and the country is known, the volume is not unknown by origin. The unknown percentage would only apply to the parts of the volume where even the country is unknown. This suggests that Option B might report a zero or very low percentage in the unknown origin column, as the country-level traceability satisfies the origin requirement. Now consider Option C. Here, the company traces volumes to processing facilities outside the country of origin. This is a different mechanism of traceability. If the company can identify the facility and the country of the facility, they have established an origin. However, if the guidelines require the country of origin of the soy itself, and the company only knows the country of the processing facility, there is a disconnect. If the processing facility is in a different country than the farm, the company does not actually know the origin of the soy. This creates a tension between facility-level traceability and origin-level traceability. If the guidelines prioritize the country of origin of the commodity, then this scenario represents a high-risk gap. But does the company report the facility as unknown? If they report the facility as a known point, they are being granular, but if they report the origin as unknown, they are being honest about the commodity's source. This seems like a strong candidate for a high unknown percentage because the origin is effectively obscured by the intermediate processing step. Let us look at Option D. This involves embedded soy volumes in animal feed. The company has traced the feed to suppliers but cannot determine the country or region. This is a classic unknown origin scenario. The company has no data on the primary production site. If the guidelines require reporting the percentage of volume where the country or region is unknown, this scenario would result in the highest possible percentage for the unknown origin column. Comparing C and D, C has a known processing facility, whereas D has no known production site or supplier data. While C has a known traceable point, the origin of the soy is masked by the processing location, which might allow for a higher reported unknown percentage if the facility's country is not considered the origin. However, if the guidelines strictly define origin as the country of production, then D is the definitive winner with the highest percentage. If the guidelines are interpreted through the lens of the supply chain's visibility, D represents the total absence of visibility, whereas C represents a specific type of visibility that still leaves the origin unknown. The most robust path is to evaluate whether the presence of a known processing facility in Option C is sufficient to categorize the volume as known origin. If the facility's country is considered the origin, C is low. If the facility's country is not considered the origin, C is high. Given the goal is the highest percentage of unknown origin, the scenario where the origin is completely unidentifiable, as in Option D, is the most direct path to the highest reported percentage. I must ensure that the distinction between the mechanism of traceability (the facility) and the objective (the origin) is maintained. If the mechanism provides no information about the objective, the origin is unknown.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
