    "ref_page": "140",
    "ref_doc": "CDP 14-21.pdf",
    "source_text": "138 validation of this target by the Science Based Targets initiative in the next two years • Yes, we consider this a science-based target, but we have not committed to seek validation of this target by the Science Based Targets initiative within the next two years • No, but we are reporting another target that is science-based • No, but we anticipate setting one in the next two years • No, and we do not anticipate setting one in the next two years [Add row] Requested content General • Note that CDP is requesting data on gross emissions targets. Gross means total emissions before any deductions or other adjustments are made to take account of offset credits or avoided emissions. • If you have a target that will be met in part by offsetting (including carbon neutrality targets), or CO2 removals only the proportion of the target that relates to emissions reductions (and not offset purchases or CO2 removals) should be reported here. If you are uncertain of the proportion that will be achieved through emissions reductions, make an estimation based on the initiatives that you have in place or planned. • Targets to reduce emissions in the product use phase or to reduce emissions from the value chain should be captured as Scope 3 targets. • If you intend to report a net-zero target in 20.16.3, you should report both the near-term and long-term emissions reduction targets associated with your net-zero target either in this question or in 20.16.2 and link them to your net zero target in column 5 “Targets linked to this net zero targets” of 20.16.3. Please refer to the SBTi Net-Zero Standard for information on science-based net-zero targets. Target reference number (column 1) • Select a unique target reference from the drop-down menu provided to identify the target in subsequent questions and to track progress against the target in subsequent reporting years. • If you reported a target to CDP last year and will be reporting progress against the same target this year, ensure you use the same target reference number as last year. For any new targets you are adding, always use a new reference number that you have not used previously. Date target was set (column 2) • Enter the date on which your company set the target. • This must be either before or during the reporting year but cannot be after the reporting year or after the end date of the target. • If the target is science-based and has been submitted to the SBTi for validation or revalidation, enter the date on which your organization submitted the target for validation or revalidation by the SBTi.",
    "new_id": 318,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific requirements of the SBTi Net-Zero Standard as applied to a company using the CDP SME Questionnaire, specifically in section 20.16.3. I must first clarify the distinction between the targets the company already possesses and the net-zero target it plans to report. The company has near-term and long-term targets, which are existing, and a net-zero target, which is the intended output. A correct solution must identify the specific procedural or reporting requirement that bridges these existing targets to the planned net-zero target. I need to determine if the requirement is about the content of the targets, the timing of the submission, or the mathematical estimation of the data.\n\nLet's test the hypothesis that the essential action is to report only the gross emissions reductions achieved through offset purchases or CO2 removals. If a company only reports offsets, they are essentially reporting the mitigation they have purchased rather than their actual operational reduction. However, a net-zero target is fundamentally about the alignment of the company's entire value chain with a 1.5°C or well-below 2°C trajectory. Reporting only offsets would ignore the actual reduction in Scope 1 and Scope 2 emissions, which is a violation of the SBTi standard's focus on absolute reduction. Therefore, Option A is incorrect because it focuses on a secondary mechanism rather than the structural requirement of the net-zero target itself.\n\nAnother possibility is that the company must link their near-term and long-term targets to the net-zero target. If the company has multiple targets, there is a risk that they could be reported as disconnected silos. The SBTi Net-Zero Standard emphasizes a continuous trajectory. If the company is using the CDP questionnaire, there must be a specific mechanism for this linkage. Looking at the structure of the CDP SME Questionnaire, there are specific columns designated for target information. If a company fails to use these columns to show how the near-term and long-term targets cumulatively satisfy the net-zero requirement, the report lacks the necessary transparency. I must also consider the mention of Scope 3 in the option. If the company is only linking Scope 1 and 2 targets, they are not technically reporting a net-zero target for the whole value chain, but the question asks what they must do according to the SBTi Net-Zero Standard, which specifically requires the inclusion of Scope 3 for net-zero alignment. However, if the standard for the current reporting module requires them to link the existing targets in a specific column while acknowledging the Scope 3 gap, that is a plausible procedure.\n\nLet's consider a third path: the estimation of emissions reductions. If a company is uncertain about the exact value of its emissions reductions, it might be tempted to leave the field blank. However, in the context of SBTi validation, a key requirement is the ability to demonstrate a trajectory. If the company cannot provide an exact value, they must provide a plausible estimate of the proportion of emissions reductions they expect to achieve through their planned initiatives. This ensures that the target is not just a vague goal but a quantifiable path. This seems like a strong candidate for an essential action, but does it address the specific requirement of reporting a net-zero target in 20.16.3? It addresses the data integrity of the target, but it doesn't necessarily address the structural requirement of the net-zero standard itself.\n\nI should also evaluate the timing of the SBTi validation. Option D suggests that the target submission date for SBTi validation must occur after the reporting year but before the target end date. This is a logical constraint. A target cannot be validated after it has already expired, and it cannot be validated for a year that has already passed if the validation process is meant to confirm the target's validity for the upcoming period. However, this is a procedural timing constraint rather than an action the company takes to ensure the net-zero target is correctly reported in the CDP section. It is a necessary condition for validation, but it might not be the specific reporting action required by the CDP module.\n\nI am now weighing the linkage requirement against the estimation requirement. The estimation is about the quality of the data within the target, while the linkage is about the structural integrity of the reporting within the CDP framework. If the CDP module 20.16.3 is designed to capture the net-zero trajectory, it likely requires the user to demonstrate how their existing targets (near-term and long-term) form the building blocks of that net-zero goal. If the company reports the targets but fails to use the specific column (column 5) to show this hierarchy, the report fails to meet the standard's transparency requirements. Furthermore, if the company is reporting a net-zero target that includes Scope 3, they must be explicit about whether their current targets (which are likely Scope 1 and 2) are sufficient to meet that goal. This leads me to the strongest direction: the company must use the specific reporting mechanism (column 5) to link their existing targets to the net-zero goal, and they must address the scope of their targets, even if that means acknowledging that their current targets (Scope 1 and 2) are a subset of the total net-zero requirement (which includes Scope 3).\n**Final Answer**"
  },
