    "source_text": "146 relative to base year [auto-calculated] Numerical field [enter a number from 0-999,999,999,999 using a maximum of 10 decimal places and no commas] Percentage field Select from: • New • Underway • Achieved • Achieved and maintained • Expired • Revised • Replaced • Retired Select from: • Yes, and this target has been approved by the Science Based Targets initiative • Yes, we consider this a science-based target, and the target is currently being reviewed by the Science Based Targets initiative • Yes, we consider this a science-based target, and we have committed to seek validation of this target by the Science Based Targets initiative in the next two years • Yes, we consider this a science-based target, but we have not committed to seek validation of this target by the Science Based Targets initiative within the next two years • No, but we are reporting another target that is science-based • No, but we anticipate setting one in the next two years • No, and we do not anticipate setting one in the next two years [Attachment(s)] Text field [maximum 5,000 characters] [Add row] Intensity metric (column 7) • Grams CO2e per revenue passenger kilometer • Metric tons CO2e per USD($) value-added • Metric tons CO2e per square meter • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of aluminum • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of steel • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of cement • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of cardboard • Grams CO2e per kilometer\n\n[Page 149]\n147 • Metric tons CO2e per unit revenue • Metric tons CO2e per unit FTE employee • Metric tons CO2e per unit hour worked • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of product • Metric tons of CO2e per liter of product • Metric tons CO2e per unit of production • Metric tons CO2e per unit of service provided • Metric tons CO2e per square foot • Metric tons CO2e per kilometer • Metric tons CO2e per passenger kilometer • Metric tons CO2e per megawatt hour (MWh) • Metric tons CO2e per barrel of oil equivalent (BOE) • Metric tons CO2e per vehicle produced • Metric tons CO2e per metric ton of ore processed • Metric tons CO2e per ounce of gold • Metric tons CO2e per ounce of platinum • Metric tons of CO2e per metric ton of aggregate • Metric tons of CO2e per billion (currency) funds under management • Other, please specify Requested content General • Note that CDP is requesting data on gross emissions targets. Gross means total emissions before any deductions or other adjustments are made to take account of offset credits or avoided emissions. • If you have a target that will be met in part by offsetting (including carbon neutrality targets), or CO2 removals only the proportion of the target that relates to emissions reductions (and not offset purchases or CO2 removals) should be reported here. If you are uncertain of the proportion that will be achieved through emissions reductions, make an estimation based on the initiatives that you have in place or planned. • Targets to reduce emissions in the product use phase or to reduce emissions from the value chain should be captured as Scope 3 targets. • If you intend to report a net-zero target in 20.16.3, you should report both the near-term and long-term emissions reduction targets associated with your net-zero target either in this question or in 20.16.1 and link them to your net zero target in column 5 “Targets linked to this net zero targets” of 20.16.3. Please refer to the SBTi Net-Zero Standard for information on science-based net-zero targets. Target Reference Number (column 1) • Select a unique target reference from the drop-down menu provided to identify the target in subsequent questions and to track progress against the target in subsequent reporting years. • If you reported a target to CDP last year and will be reporting progress against the same target this year, ensure you use the same target reference number as last year. For any new targets you are adding, always use a new reference number that you have not used previously. Date target was set (column 2) • Enter the date on which your company set the target. • This must be either before or during the reporting year but cannot be after the reporting year or after the end date of the target.",
    "new_id": 333,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific guidance from the CDP SME Questionnaire for April 2025 regarding Modules 14-21. I need to determine if a company can report a single aggregate figure for a partially achieved target or if it is required to bifurcate the results into two distinct categories: emissions reductions and offsets. The ambiguity stems from whether the guidance treats offsets as a valid component of progress toward the target or as a separate, secondary mechanism. I must clarify what constitutes a correct solution. A correct solution must explain the mandatory reporting structure for a target that is not fully met through direct operational reductions. It must address the distinction between the objective of the target and the mechanism used to reach it.\n\nLet's start with a hypothesis that the company can report a unified figure. If the target is to reduce total emissions by a certain amount, and the company uses a mix of internal reductions and offsets to reach that amount, the total is the total. Reporting the sum of reductions and offsets as a single achievement seems like the most straightforward way to communicate progress to a stakeholder. However, I must test this against the concept of materiality and transparency. If a stakeholder is interested in the company's actual decarbonization performance—meaning the reduction in the company's direct carbon footprint—reporting the total including offsets might be misleading. An offset is a financial instrument or a project in another location, whereas a reduction is a change in the company's own emissions profile. If the guidance is designed to provide high-quality, comparable ESG data, a lump-sum approach might fail to meet the transparency requirements for material environmental metrics.\n\nLet's pivot to a different path. Perhaps the guidance requires a distinction based on the nature of the data. The problem mentions that reductions might be based on estimations or planned initiatives. This is a crucial piece of information. If the reductions are not yet realized and are based on planned initiatives, they are fundamentally different from verified offsets. If I were to report them all together, the reliability of the data would be compromised. If I report the reductions as planned and the offsets as realized, I am providing a hybrid of projections and certainties. This suggests that the reporting structure must account for the different levels of certainty in the two components.\n\nI should consider if the company has a choice in the matter. If the guidance is prescriptive, then \"optionality\" is not a valid path. If the guidance says \"must,\" then any \"should\" or \"may\" response is incorrect. Let's test the idea that offsets are the primary metric. If a company reports only the offset component, they are essentially saying that their progress is entirely driven by external purchases. This would ignore the \"reductions\" part of the target. If the target specifically includes a requirement for internal decarbonization, then treating offsets as the primary metric would be a violation of the target's intent. Therefore, option D is likely incorrect because it elevates the offset above the reduction, which contradicts the goal of most ESG frameworks that prioritize actual emission reductions.\n\nWhat about the \"not sure\" option? In formal reporting, \"not sure\" is not a reporting methodology; it is an admission of lack of knowledge. If the guidance is clear, the company cannot claim uncertainty as a reporting strategy. I will reject Z as it does not address the structural requirement of the question.\n\nThis leaves me with a tension between a simple total and a granular breakdown. If the target is partially achieved, there is a mathematical reality: the total progress is the sum of the reduction and the offset. But the reporting requirement might be about the *structure* of that report. If the guidance requires transparency into the \"how\" of the progress, then the company cannot simply report a single number. If the reductions are based on planned initiatives, they are inherently less concrete than the offsets. This implies that the reporting must distinguish between the two to prevent the conflation of actual operational change with financial compensation. \n\nI need to test the \"separate reporting\" hypothesis against the constraint of the \"partially achieved\" status. If the target is 100 units and the company has 40 units of reduction (20 planned + 20 realized) and 30 units of offset, the total progress is 70. But the question asks how they *must* treat the two under the guidance. If the guidance requires transparency into the composition of the progress, then the company must report the gross emissions and then break down the 70 into its constituent parts: the realized reductions and the offsets. This would mean the company is not just reporting a single \"achieved\" number, but is instead providing a breakdown of the mechanisms used to reach that number. This distinguishes the \"reductions\" as a distinct metric from the \"offsets\" as a distinct metric, while still acknowledging the total progress made toward the target. This approach satisfies the need for transparency regarding the reliability of the data (distinguishing planned vs. realized) and the distinction between the company's own footprint reduction and its compensatory actions. The strongest direction involves a requirement to report the components separately to ensure clarity on the source of the progress.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
