    "ref_doc": "CDP 14-21.pdf",
    "source_text": "124 • Your selection in this column should align with the previous column, “Allocation method”. For example, if “Allocation based on the area of products purchased” is selected, the unit may be cubic feet, cubic meters, square meters, or hectares. Or, if “Allocation based on the market value of products purchased” is selected, the unit will be “Currency” o If “currency” is selected, the figure provided in column 8 “Market value or quantity of goods/services supplied to the requesting member” should be in the same currency that you selected in question 14.2 for all financial information disclosed throughout your response. Market value or quantity of goods/services supplied to the requesting member (numerator) (column 8) • Report the unit of goods/services provided to the customer. • If you provide multiple goods/services that do not share a common unit, provide the market value of the goods and/or services supplied. Emissions in metric tons of CO2e (column 9) • Specify the metric tons of CO2e you are allocating to your customer for the scope given in column 2 “Scope of emissions”. Major sources of emissions (column 11) • Describe significant sources of emissions for which you have provided a figure. The following list of examples is non-exhaustive: o Scope 1 emissions may be equipment in which fuel is burnt to provide heat (e.g. ovens, driers or kilns); emissions from organization owned or controlled vehicles; emissions from production processes e.g. in cement manufacture; o Scope 2 emissions may include electricity used to power production lines, lighting in offices, electricity for data centers, etc.; and o Scope 3 covers a broader range of possible sources. For example, the “Scope 3, Business travel” category would include air travel for organization employees; the “Scope 3, Capital goods” category would include the manufacture of steel to make heavy machinery or infrastructure; and the “Scope 3, Waste generated in operations category” would include emissions from out-sourced treatment of organic waste. Please explain how you have identified the GHG source, including major limitations to this process and assumptions made (column 13) • Organizations often have many different sources of emissions and this question seeks to understand how you have selected major emission sources. • The GHG Protocol Corporate Standard states companies should report on all emissions within their chosen organizational boundary. This defines the sources of emissions on which you are going to report. There are three options: sources in which the organization has an equity share; sources over which the organization has financial control; sources over which the company has operational control. If you exclude any sources within the boundary, you are asked to disclose and justify those exclusions. • However, it may be that you have been limited by your knowledge of potential emission sources or made assumptions about which sources were the largest. Or alternatively, that certain sources do not play a role for the specific products your customers are purchasing from you. Please explain the thinking behind your selection including the difficulties that you encountered. Where published information has been used, please provide a reference(s) (column 14) • To allocate emissions to your customer you may have used your own (primary) data in answering this question. Alternatively, you may have relied on publications that give industry-average (secondary) data for particular materials or processes or you may have used a mixture of both. In order to make the origin of the data clear, provide references where published information has been used, as well as flag where they have been used.",
    "new_id": 344,
    "attempt": "A",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in understanding the specific logic used in the CDP SME Questionnaire for 2025 to determine which emission sources are material enough to be included in a report. I need to clarify what is meant by major sources. Does this refer to the absolute magnitude of the emission, the proportion of the total carbon footprint, or the strategic importance of the activity to the business? The question asks for the reasoning behind exclusions, which implies I am looking for the threshold or the criteria that, when not met, allow a source to be omitted. A correct solution must explain the specific metric or condition that justifies an omission according to the methodology described in the questionnaire.\n\nLet's start by testing a hypothesis based on absolute impact. Perhaps the methodology uses a hard tonnage threshold, such as excluding any source producing less than 1,000 tonnes of CO2e. If this were the case, a small office building's emissions might be ignored even if they are a significant part of the total for a micro-enterprise. However, this seems unlikely because carbon reporting standards like those used by CDP are typically relative to the total footprint of the reporting entity. An absolute threshold would be too rigid and would not account for the scale of different organizations. I will reject the absolute tonnage idea because it fails to address the concept of materiality, which is central to ESG reporting.\n\nLet's pivot to a different path: the concept of materiality based on the value chain. Maybe the reasoning for exclusion is that if a source is not relevant to the products or services sold to a specific customer, it is excluded. This would mean the methodology is customer-driven. I will test this by considering a company that has high emissions from its corporate headquarters but sells a digital service where the customer cares only about the energy used during transmission. Under this hypothesis, the headquarters emissions would be excluded. But wait, that doesn't make sense for a corporate ESG report; a company is responsible for its own footprint, not just what the customer sees. This hypothesis fails because it confuses the scope of the product's impact with the scope of the company's operational responsibility. The methodology must be looking at the company's total footprint, not just the customer's view.\n\nWhat if the exclusion is based on the difficulty of data collection? If the data is unavailable or too difficult to obtain, the source is excluded. This would mean the methodology is data-driven. Let's test this. If a company leases a shared facility and cannot get data from the landlord, they might exclude it. But is this a valid reason? Most ESG frameworks, including those in the CDP, actually encourage estimation or proxy use rather than simple exclusion when data is hard to get. If the methodology allowed for easy exclusion based on data unavailability, the reports would be full of gaps. This seems like a weak path because it contradicts the general trend toward completeness in standardized reporting.\n\nLet's reconsider the proportion idea, but this time let's look at it as a mathematical threshold rather than an absolute one. If the methodology defines a major source as one that contributes significantly to the total, then the reasoning for exclusion must be the lack of significance. If a source contributes less than a certain percentage, it is deemed a minor contributor. I need to test if this percentage is a fixed number, like 5%. If a source is below this threshold, its inclusion would not meaningfully change the total reported figure. This makes sense because the goal of an ESG report is to provide a material view of the risks and impacts. If a source is a tiny fraction of the total, it might be excluded to maintain the focus on the most impactful areas. \n\nLet's test a counterexample to see if this holds up. Suppose a company has 95% of its emissions in one category and 5% in another. If the methodology uses a 5% threshold, the 5% category is excluded. Is this a problem? If the 5% represents a massive, high-risk activity, like a small but intense chemical processing unit, excluding it would be a failure of the report. However, the question asks for the reasoning behind why certain sources might be excluded based on the methodology, not whether the methodology is perfect. The methodology itself provides a rule: if the contribution is below a certain level, the source is not considered a major source. \n\nI should also look at the distinction between operational control and financial control. Could the exclusion be based on the level of control the company has? If a company has equity in a joint venture but no operational control, they might exclude it. But the question asks about the source of emissions, not the accounting method. While there is a link between what you control and what you report, the primary driver for excluding a source should be tied to the materiality of the emission itself, not the legal structure of the ownership. Therefore, the control-based approach is a secondary mechanism and likely not the primary reasoning for why a source is excluded based on its impact.\n\nI have rejected the absolute tonnage, the customer-relevance, and the data-availability hypotheses because they either ignore the scale of the company or conflict with the principle of seeking a complete material picture. The most robust direction is the materiality threshold, specifically a percentage-based approach. This explains how a source can be \"real\" but still be \"excluded\" because its impact is negligible compared to the total. I need to focus on how this threshold defines the boundary between what is considered a \"major\" source and what is considered \"minor\" for the purpose of the report.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
