    "source_text": "14 1938Chapter 14 North America High-humidity hazards are projected to increase (medium confidence) in regions around the Gulf of Mexico and southeast North America (US-SE, US-SP , MX-NE, MX-SE) (Zhao et al., 2015). In subtropical regions that are less influenced by moisture from the Gulf of Mexico (including US-SW, US-SP , MX-NW and MX-N), the combination of higher temperature and less total precipitation leads to projections of increased aridity: drier surface conditions, higher evaporative demand by plants and more intense droughts (Ranasinghe et al., 2021; Jones and Gutzler, 2016; Easterling et al., 2017; Escalante-Sandoval and Nuñez-Garcia, 2017). As temperatures rise, snow extent, duration of snow cover and accumulated snowpack are virtually certain to decline in subarctic regions of North America (Gutierrez et al., 2021a; McCrary and Mearns, 2019; Mudryk et al., 2021), with corresponding effects on snow- related hydrological changes (high confidence). These changes include declines in snowmelt runoff (Li et al., 2017), increased evaporative losses during snow ablation (Foster et al., 2016; Milly and Dunne, 2020), as well as increases in the frequency of rain-on-snow events (Jeong and Sushama, 2018a) and consecutive snow drought years in western North America (Marshall et al., 2019a). Climate change is projected to magnify the impact of tropical cyclones in US-NE, MX-NE, US-SP , and US-SE by increasing rainfall (Patricola and Wehner, 2018) and extreme wind speed (high confidence) and slowing the speed of land-falling storms (limited evidence, low confidence) (Seneviratne et al., 2021; Kossin, 2018). The coastal region at severe risk from tropical storms is projected to expand northward within US- NE (medium confidence) (Kossin et al., 2017).Additional reduction in polar sea ice is virtually certain (Ranasinghe et al., 2021; Mudryk et al., 2021), with the North American Arctic projected to be seasonally ice free at least once per decade under 2°C of global warming (high confidence) (IPCC, 2019b; Mioduszewski et al., 2019; Mudryk et al., 2018). Duration of freshwater lake ice across the northern USA and southern Canada is projected to diminish (high confidence) (Ranasinghe et al., 2021; Dibike et al., 2012; Mudryk et al., 2018; Sharma et al., 2019). Ocean surface temperature is very likely to increase in future decades in waters around North America (Jewett and Romanou, 2017; Greenan et al., 2018), but at a slower rate than air temperature over the continent. Rates of change are projected to be relatively higher in northern latitudes, with most rapid warming in summer in the Arctic and Bering Sea (US-AK, CA-NW) (Wang and Overland, 2015; Wang et al., 2018a; Hermann et al., 2019). Sea level rise is virtually certain to continue along North American coastlines except for parts of US-AK and around Hudson Bay (HB) with geographically variable rates of rise (Fox-Kemper et al., 2021; Ranasinghe et al., 2021; see Box 14.4). Relatively greater SLR is projected along the US-SE and MX-SW coastlines and relatively less along CA-BC and US-NW (Fox-Kemper et al., 2021; Ranasinghe et al., 2021; see Box 14.4) (Fasullo and Nerem, 2018; Greenan et al., 2018 IPCC, 2019b). Ocean acidification (OA) along North American coastlines is projected to increase (very high confidence) (Jewett and Romanou, 2017). The frequency and extent of oxygen minimum and hypoxic zones are Frequently Asked Questions FAQ 14.1 | How has climate change contributed to recent extreme events in North America and their impacts? Multiple lines of evidence indicate that climate change is already contributing to more intense and more frequent extreme events across North America. The impacts resulting from extreme events represent a huge challenge for adapting to future climate change. Extreme events are a fundamental part of how we experience weather and climate. Exceptionally hot days, torrential rainfall and other extreme weather events have a direct impact on people, communities and ecosystems. Extreme weather can lead to other impactful events such as droughts, floods or wildfires. In a changing climate, people frequently ask whether extreme events are generally becoming more severe or more frequent, and whether an actual extreme event was caused by climate change. Because really extreme events occur rarely (by definition), it can be very difficult to assess whether the overall severity or frequency of such events has been affected by changing climate. Neverthel",
    "new_id": 386,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in understanding the causal link between a specific atmospheric condition—the lack of moisture from the Gulf of Mexico—and a specific climatic outcome—increased aridity—in subtropical regions. I must first clarify what aridity means in this context; it is not merely the absence of rainfall, but a state where the demand for water exceeds the supply. The term subtropical is also significant, as these regions typically sit on the poleward edge of a major moisture source, making them inherently prone to shifts in precipitation patterns. A correct solution must explain how rising temperatures interact with the specific hydrological constraints of these regions to produce a drier environment. I need to determine if the aridity is driven by a reduction in water input, an increase in water loss, or both.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis presented in Option A: Higher temperatures lead to reduced snowpack, which decreases water availability for these regions. While this is a plausible mechanism for aridity in mountainous or high-latitude environments, it may be a weak explanation for subtropical regions. Subtropical regions are generally defined by their distance from the polar ice caps and their position relative to large moisture sources. If the region is specifically described as being less influenced by the Gulf of Mexico, the hydrological cycle is likely driven more by direct solar heating and seasonal winds than by seasonal snowpack. Therefore, while temperature rise will certainly affect snowmelt, the primary driver of aridity in these specific subtropical zones is more likely tied to the moisture availability from the adjacent ocean rather than the presence or absence of snow. I will reject Option A because it focuses on a mechanism that is secondary to the regional climate drivers described.\n\nI will now consider Option C: Increased frequency of tropical cyclones results in more rapid evaporation and soil desiccation. This is an interesting but problematic hypothesis. Tropical cyclones are indeed moisture-laden systems, and an increase in their frequency would generally be expected to increase precipitation and soil moisture in the long term. The mechanism of rapid evaporation due to stronger winds might lead to temporary soil desiccation, but it does not explain a long-term trend of increased aridity. If the frequency of these storms increases, the net effect should be an increase in moisture, which contradicts the premise of the question. Furthermore, the IPCC report focuses heavily on the relationship between warming and the hydrological cycle, often highlighting how increased aridity is a result of drying rather than increased storminess. I must reject Option C because the mechanism it proposes actually works against the observed trend of increasing aridity.\n\nThis leaves me to evaluate the relationship between temperature and the two primary components of the hydrological cycle: precipitation and evaporation. Let us look at Option B: Decreased total precipitation and higher evaporative demand combine to create drier surface conditions. This is a dual-mechanism approach. The first part, decreased total precipitation, addresses the supply side. If the Gulf of Mexico moisture transport is disrupted or weakened, the total volume of water entering the system decreases. The second part, higher evaporative demand, addresses the loss side. As global temperatures rise, the atmosphere's capacity to hold moisture increases, following the Clausius-Clapeyron relation. This means that for every degree of temperature increase, the potential for evaporation increases, creating a powerful force for drying. The combination of less water coming in and more water being pulled out by the atmosphere creates a net loss, which is the definition of increased aridity. This hypothesis is robust because it directly links the specific atmospheric condition (less Gulf moisture) to the global driver (rising temperatures) and explains the output (aridity) through a clear physical mechanism involving both supply and demand.\n\nI must also briefly consider if Option D provides a better explanation: Slower-moving land-falling storms cause prolonged dry periods between precipitation events. While slower-moving storms can change the temporal pattern of precipitation, they do not necessarily change the total volume of moisture available to the region in a way that explains a systemic shift toward aridity. A dry period is a temporal phenomenon, whereas aridity is a state of the environment. If the total amount of moisture remains the same but is just delivered in more intense, less frequent bursts, the region might experience flooding and droughts, but the overall aridity might not increase. This mechanism is too narrow; it describes a change in the rhythm of precipitation rather than a fundamental shift in the balance between water input and loss. I will reject Option D.\n\nThe most rigorous path is to examine the interplay between the reduction in moisture transport from the Gulf of Mexico and the intensifying evaporative demand caused by global warming. This approach explains how a decrease in precipitation can be exacerbated by an increase in evaporation, leading to the observed increase in aridity. The strength of this direction lies in its ability to connect the specific regional driver (Gulf moisture) with the global driver (temperature) through the physical mechanisms of precipitation and evaporation.\n**Final Answer**"
