  {
    "id": 29590,
    "question": "Which statement accurately reflects the conditions under which an entity must disclose water-related metrics in locations with High or Extremely High Baseline Water Stress, as described in the Construction Materials – Sustainability Accounting Standard?",
    "options": {
      "A": "The entity must disclose both water withdrawn and water consumed as percentages of their respective totals in such locations.",
      "B": "The entity must disclose water consumed as a percentage of total water withdrawn in such locations.",
      "C": "The entity is required to disclose only water withdrawn, without considering water consumption, in such locations.",
      "D": "The entity must calculate water stress levels independently, without relying on the WRI’s Aqueduct tool classifications.",
      "Z": "Not sure"
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "ref_page": "17-18",
    "ref_doc": "SASB Construction Materials.pdf",
    "source_text": "4The entity shall analyse all its operations for water risks and identify activities that withdraw and consume water in locations with High (40 –80%) or Extremely High (>80%) Baseline Water Stress as classified by the World Resources Institute ’s (WRI) Water Risk Atlas tool, Aqueduct. 5The entity shall disclose water withdrawn in locations with High or Extremely High Baseline Water Stress as a percentage of the total water withdrawn. 6The entity shall disclose water consumed in locations with High or Extremely High Baseline Water Stress as a percentage of the total water consumed. SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |CONSTRUCTION MATERIALS |17\n\n[Page 18]\nWaste Management Topic Summary Construction materials production recycling rates are high. However, waste from production processes, pollution control devices and from hazardous waste management activities present a regulatory risk and can increase operating costs. Cement kiln dust (CKD) —consisting of fine-grained, solid, highly alkaline waste removed from cement kiln exhaust gas by air pollution control devices —is the most significant waste category in the industry. Regulatory risk remains high from evolving environmental laws. Entities that reduce waste streams —hazardous waste streams in particular —and recycle by-products, can reduce regulatory and litigation risks and costs. Metrics EM-CM-150a.1. Amount of waste generated, percentage hazardous and percentage recycled 1 The entity shall disclose the amount of waste generated in metric tonnes. 1.1 Waste is defined as anything for which the entity has no further use, and which is discarded or released to the environment. 1.2 The scope includes slags, dusts, sludges, used oil and other solid wastes that meet the above definition. 1.3 The scope excludes gaseous waste. 2The entity shall disclose the percentage of waste generated that was hazardous. 2.1 The percentage of hazardous waste shall be calculated as the weight hazardous waste as defined in accordance with the applicable jurisdictional, legal or regulatory framework where the waste was generated divided by the total weight of waste material. 2.2 Hazardous waste generally displays the following characteristics: ignitability, corrosivity, reactivity or toxicity. 2.3 The entity may use United Nations Environmental Programme (UNEP) Basel Convention on the Control of Transboundary Movements of Hazardous Wastes and Their Disposal for the purposes of defining hazardous waste for operations located in jurisdictions that lack applicable legal or regulatory definitions. 3 The entity shall disclose the percentage of waste generated that was recycled. 3.1 The percentage recycled shall be calculated as the weight of waste material reused, plus the weight recycled or remanufactured (through treatment or processing) by the entity, plus the amount sent externally for further recycling, divided by the total weight of waste material. 3.1.1 Reused materials are defined as those recovered products or components of products used for the same purpose for which they were conceived. SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |CONSTRUCTION MATERIALS |18",
    "new_id": 393,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific disclosure requirements triggered by a geographic indicator of water scarcity. I must first clarify the ambiguous terms. What does water consumed versus water withdrawn represent in a construction materials context? Withdrawn typically refers to the total volume taken from a source, while consumed refers to the portion that is not returned to the original source, often due to evaporation, incorporation into a product, or loss during processing. The term baseline water stress is a technical metric, likely an external input, which distinguishes the trigger from the actual calculation method. A correct solution must explain the specific ratio that is mandated and how the stress level dictates the reporting obligation.\n\nI will first test the hypothesis that the disclosure requirement is a simple binary based on the stress level. If the location is High or Extremely High, perhaps the entity must simply report all water data. However, this is a weak hypothesis because ESG standards usually demand specific mathematical rigor rather than broad qualitative reporting. If a standard required all water data in stressed areas, it would be less precise than a ratio-based requirement. I must look for a specific metric.\n\nLet's consider a second path: the requirement is a volumetric measurement of total withdrawal. This would mean the entity must report the absolute volume of water withdrawn in these areas. I will test this against the provided options. If the standard only required the volume of water withdrawn, Option B and Option A would be incorrect because they involve percentages and consumption. However, if I look at the logic of water stress, reporting only the volume is less useful for comparing scarcity impact than reporting a ratio of what is actually being lost to the system. Therefore, a volumetric-only approach is likely insufficient to meet the objective of the standard.\n\nThis leads me to a third path: the requirement is a ratio involving consumption. If the standard requires a ratio, I need to determine if it is a ratio of consumption to withdrawal or a ratio of withdrawal to total water. Let's test the logic of a consumption-to-withdrawal ratio. In a high-stress area, this ratio would represent the efficiency of water use. If an entity reports this, it provides a direct measure of the local water depletion impact. Now, I must check if the standard requires this ratio to be expressed as a percentage. If the standard requires a percentage, then the denominator must be the total water withdrawn. This would mean the entity is reporting the proportion of the total water intake that is actually consumed.\n\nWait, I must be careful not to confuse the mechanism with the output. The mechanism is the calculation of the ratio, and the output is the percentage. The trigger is the WRI’s Aqueduct tool classification of High or Extremely High baseline water stress. Does the entity have to calculate the stress level itself? Option D suggests they must calculate it independently. If the standard relies on the WRI classification, then the entity does not have to perform the complex hydrological modeling required by Aqueduct; they just have to use the existing classification as their trigger. This makes the procedure more manageable for a reporting entity.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the options with this refined logic. Option A suggests reporting both withdrawn and consumed water as percentages of their respective totals. This sounds complex and might be a distraction. If the standard wanted both, it would likely specify two separate metrics. Option B is more streamlined: it requires reporting water consumed as a percentage of total water withdrawn. This aligns with the logic of expressing consumption as a proportion of the total intake. Option C is incorrect because it ignores the consumption component entirely, which would not provide a meaningful measure of water impact in a stressed area. \n\nI must test the strength of Option B against the constraint of the stress trigger. The requirement is conditional on the Aqueduct classification. Therefore, the procedure is: identify the location's stress level using Aqueduct, and if it is High or Extremely High, then calculate the consumption as a percentage of withdrawal. This provides a standardized way to compare how different entities impact water-scarce regions. The strongest direction is to focus on this specific percentage-based ratio as the required output for locations identified by the WRI tool.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
