rriers to implementation. The most frequent complaint in these reports is the difficulty of data acquisition and the resulting financial burden. If the report uses the phrase \"shared concern\" to describe the financial and data-related hurdles, and it specifically points out that the current recommendations do not solve the \"how\" of the data collection, the strongest direction is the cost of data collection. I will focus on testing whether the \"cost\" option satisfies all parts of the question: shared across case studies and surveys, mentioned in the 2023 report, and not directly addressed by the recommendations. The tension here is between the necessity of data and the absence of a cost-effective way to obtain it. If the recommendations focus on the \"what\" and \"why\" but not the \"how\" of the financial burden, the gap is clearly the cost.\n**Final Answer**"
