    "ref_doc": "SDG 2024.pdf",
    "source_text": "13Declines in maternal mortality have stalled, with wide disparities by region and income Between 2015 and 2020, the global maternal mortality ratio declined only marginally from 227 to 223 maternal deaths per 100,000 live births, over three times higher than the 2030 target of 70. Sub-Saharan Africa and Southern Asia account for 87 per cent of maternal deaths. In 2020, the maternal mortality ratio in low-income countries was 430 maternal deaths per 100,000 live births compared to 13 in high-income nations. The average maternal mortality ratio for very high and high alert fragile states was 551 in 2020, more than twice the global average. Achieving the 2030 target requires an annual reduction rate of 11.6 per cent between 2021 and 2030.Skilled birth attendance has improved globally, rising from 80 per cent of births in 2015 to 86 per cent in 2023. Improved care quality could save the lives of up to 1 million newborns and 150,000 mothers annually. Y et progress is stalling; 18 million births occurred without skilled assistance in 2023. In sub-Saharan Africa, only 73 per cent of births were attended by skilled personnel in 2023, the lowest rate worldwide. In contrast, Eastern and South-Eastern Asia achieved a rate of 96 per cent. Goal 3 | Good health and well-beingImprovements in adolescent birth rates and access to modern contraceptives do not benefit all women and girls equally The adolescent birth rate for women aged 15–19 years declined to 41.3 births per 1,000 women in 2023, from 47.2 in 2015 and 64.5 in 2000. For girls aged 10–14 years, the rate fell from 3.3 births per 1,000 girls in 2000 to 1.8 in 2015 and 1.5 in 2023. Y et 13.1 million babies, approximately 10 per cent of the total number of births, were born to adolescent mothers in 2023. Central and Southern Asia witnessed the largest drop-offs in adolescent birth rates between 2000 and 2023, with a 72 per cent reduction in the 15–19 age group and a 90 per cent decrease in the 10–14 cohort. Sub-Saharan Africa continued to have the highest rates globally, at 97.9 births per 1,000 women aged 15–19 and 4.4 per 1,000 girls aged 10–14 in 2023. The proportion of women of reproductive age (15–49 years) who have their need for family planning satisfied with modern methods has climbed from 76.5 per cent in 2015 to 77.6 per cent in 2024, corresponding to an additional 75 million women using modern contraceptive methods globally. By 2030, this proportion is projected to reach 78.2 per cent, a net increase of 45 million women. This marks a fair improvement but still falls far short of universal access. Under-5 deaths hit a record low in 2022; achieving the SDG target could save 9 million lives by 2030 Global under-5 deaths reached a historic low of 4.9 million in 2022, down from 9.9 million in 2000 and 6.0 million in 2015. Nearly half these deaths (2.3 million) occurred during the neonatal period (the first 28 days of life). The under-5 mortality rate was 37 deaths per 1,000 live births in 2022, a 51 per cent reduction since 2000 and a 14 per cent reduction from 2015. The global neonatal mortality rate fell to 17 deaths per 1,000 live births in 2022, a 44 per cent reduction since 2000 and a 12 per cent reduction since 2015. Unfortunately, the annual rate of reduction in under-5 mortality contracted from 3.8 per cent in 2000–2015 to 2.1 per cent in 2015–2022. The neonatal mortality rate followed a similar pattern, declining from 3.0 per cent to 1.8 per cent. If current trends continue, an estimated 35 million children will die before reaching their fifth birthday by 2030. If all countries met the target for under-5 mortality, an estimated 9 million lives could be saved by 2030. As of 2022, 134 countries had already met the target. Seven more were on track but 59 countries, nearly three quarters of which are in sub-Saharan Africa, will need faster progress to meet the target. 97.9 51.4 49.9 35.9 26.819.711.68.141.3128.9 83.5 69.4 51.696.2 21.927.8 19.364.4 02040608010012014015–19 years 4.4 2.32.00.90.5 0.40.1 0.11.59.0 4.2 4.0 1.94.8 0.5 0.50.13.3 0246810 Sub- Saharan AfricaLatin America and the CaribbeanOceania* Northern Africa and Western AsiaCentral and Southern AsiaEastern and South- Eastern AsiaEurope and Northern AmericaAustralia and New ZealandWorld10–14 years * Excluding Australia and New Zealand.Adolescent birth rate, by age group, 2000 and 2023 (births per 1,000 women or girls in each age group) 2000 2023 Under-5 and neonatal mortality rate, 2015–2022 (deaths pe",
    "new_id": 435,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying the precise causal and descriptive relationship between two specific demographic indicators: adolescent birth rates and maternal mortality ratios, as framed by the specific data in The Sustainable Development Goals Report 2024. I must first clarify the distinction between these two variables. Adolescent birth rates are a measure of fertility or reproductive behavior among a specific age group, while maternal mortality ratios measure the risk of death during pregnancy or childbirth. A correct solution must explain how these two metrics interact or diverge according to the report's findings. I need to be careful not to assume a direct causal link exists without evidence, nor can I simply state that they are related.\n\nLet's test the hypothesis presented in Option A. It claims a direct causal relationship where a decline in adolescent birth rates leads to a proportional reduction in maternal mortality ratios. While it is logical to assume that fewer births would lead to fewer maternal deaths, this is a simplistic view. Maternal mortality is driven by a complex interplay of factors including access to healthcare, quality of medical facilities, and the presence of skilled birth attendants. If the report shows that adolescent birth rates are falling but maternal mortality remains high in certain regions, then this hypothesis fails. I must check if the report mentions any decoupling of these trends. If the trends are decoupled, the cause of the reduction in births is not the same as the cause of the reduction in mortality.\n\nMoving to Option B, I see a different causal claim. It attributes the high maternal mortality ratio in Sub-Saharan Africa specifically to its high adolescent birth rates. This is a potential error in reasoning. While high adolescent birth rates are a risk factor for maternal mortality, attributing the entire high ratio to this single factor ignores other significant drivers like the availability of obstetric care, blood transfusion services, and emergency medical access. If the report identifies systemic weaknesses in healthcare infrastructure, then the high birth rate is a contributing factor but not the sole cause. I must look for whether the report distinguishes between risk factors and primary drivers.\n\nOption C suggests a more nuanced relationship. It acknowledges a positive trend in adolescent birth rates while simultaneously noting that maternal mortality disparities remain due to factors like regional fragility and income inequality. This introduces the concept of structural determinants. If I test this against the logic of ESG reporting, it makes sense. Improving fertility rates is a social indicator, whereas maternal mortality is a health and systemic indicator. Even if the social behavior improves, the legacy of inequality and conflict can maintain high mortality rates. This seems like a much more sophisticated way to approach the data.\n\nLet's reconsider Option D. It states that universal access to modern contraceptives has been achieved, which would theoretically eliminate the correlation between adolescent births and maternal deaths. This is a bold claim. If universal access were a reality, the correlation would indeed be mitigated, but the data on contraceptive access globally is far from universal. If the report actually shows that access is still uneven, then this option is factually incorrect. Furthermore, even with perfect access to contraceptives, maternal mortality would not drop to zero because other factors like pregnancy-related complications, hemorrhage, and infection would still be present. This option is likely a distractor because it presents a perfect solution that ignores the complexity of global health statistics.\n\nI am now left with a choice between a direct causal link and a nuanced, multi-factorial approach. The direct causal link in Option A is likely too simple to be the correct interpretation of an ESG report, which usually deals with complex datasets. The regional attribution in Option B is likely too narrow to be accurate. The idea of universal access in Option D is likely incorrect given the current state of global health metrics. This leaves Option C, which suggests that while adolescent birth rates are improving, the persistent disparities in maternal mortality are driven by broader structural and regional issues. I need to verify if the report distinguishes between the trend of decreasing births and the ongoing challenge of high mortality. If the report highlights the resilience of maternal mortality despite improvements in birth rates, then Option C is the most accurate reflection of the relationship described. The distinction between the mechanism of reducing births and the outcome of reducing mortality is crucial here. The strongest direction is to look for how the report characterizes the persistence of maternal mortality as a problem that transcends simple demographic shifts.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
