    "correct_answer": "D",
    "ref_page": "38",
    "ref_doc": "SDG 2024.pdf",
    "source_text": "The Sustainable Development Goals Report 202436Life below water • Oceans face significant challenges from eutrophication, worsening acidification, declining fish stocks, rising temperatures and widespread pollution. All these factors destroy habitats, diminish biodiversity and threaten coastal communities and the health of marine ecosystems, vital to over 3 billion people. • Efforts to address these mounting concerns remain uneven. Key actions include implementing sustainable fishing practices, expanding marine protected areas to safeguard key biodiversity areas, increasing capacities to monitor ocean health and addressing the pollution that is choking waterways. • Comprehensive global action is under way, yet it must accelerate. Priorities include ensuring that the Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies enters into force as soon as possible; increasing participation in the Agreement on Port State Measures to combat illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing; adopting a global plastic pollution instrument; and ensuring that the Agreement on Marine Biodiversity of Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction enters into force as soon as possible to ensure the long-term health and sustainability of oceans. A group of bleached corals in a reef in Indonesia. Record high ocean temperatures have triggered a fourth global coral bleaching event. Overfishing, pollution, climate change and poor management drive continued declines in fish stocks The sustainability of global fishery resources declined from 90.0 per cent in 1974 to 64.6 per cent in 2019 and further to 62.3 per cent in 2021, due to overfishing, pollution, poor management and other factors. Fish stocks within biologically sustainable levels, however, comprised around 76.9 per cent of global marine fish landings in 2021. Averaging 80 million tons annually, global marine fish landings have remained relatively stable since 1995. T rends in 2021 for major fishing regions vary greatly, from 33 per cent to 84 per cent of fish stocks at sustainable levels (underfished and maximally sustainably fished). The Southeast Pacific had the highest percentage of overfished stocks at 66.7 per cent, followed by the Mediterranean and Black Sea at 62.5 per cent, the Northwest Pacific at 56 per cent and the Eastern Central Atlantic at 51.3 per cent. In contrast, the Eastern Central Pacific, Northeast Atlantic, Northeast Pacific and Southwest Pacific had the lowest proportions of overfished stocks, ranging from 16 to 24 per cent. Overfishing can harm biodiversity, ecosystems and fisheries production, and imposes adverse social and economic costs. Effective fisheries management can possibly reverse these effects (if they are driven by overfishing and not factors such as habitat degradation, pollution or climate change) and lead to optimal stock levels while supporting global food security and coastal communities. The ongoing albeit decelerated decline in biologically sustainable fish stocks worldwide underscores the need for enhanced regulatory frameworks and efficient monitoring systems. Proportion of fish stocks within biologically sustainable and biologically unsustainable levels, 1974 –2021 (percentage) 0255075100 1974 1980 1990 2000 2010 2021Maximally sustainably fished UnderfishedOverfished Note: Underfished and maximally sustainably fished stocks are considered within biologically sustainable levels. Overfished stocks are considered at biologically unsustainable levels.Proportion of fish stocks within biologically sustainable levels, by marine region, 2004 and 2021 (percentage) 56.5 65.9 90.0 77.4 70.8 70.4 56.5 85.7 88.6 94.3 75.0 89.3 68.4 63.6 81.033.3 37.5 44.0 48.7 58.0 58.8 59.5 62.7 64.3 65.2 66.7 75.9 76.5 79.4 84.2 0 20 40 60 80 100Pacific, Southeast Mediterranean and Black Sea Pacific, Northwest Atlantic, Eastern Central Atlantic, Western Central Atlantic, Southwest Atlantic, Southeast Indian Ocean, Eastern Atlantic, Northwest Pacific, Western Central Indian Ocean, Western Pacific, Southwest Pacific, North east A tlantic, Northeast Pacific, Eastern Central 2004 2021",
    "new_id": 436,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying which factors are both within the scope of fisheries management and capable of driving the recovery of fish stocks. I must first clarify what is meant by effective fisheries management in this context. It implies a set of tools including quotas, gear restrictions, protected areas, and monitoring protocols. The term declines in fish stocks is the target outcome, and the goal is to find the causal factors that management can actually act upon. I need to be careful to distinguish between drivers that are purely biological, purely environmental, and those that are resource-extraction related. A correct solution must identify the specific pressures that, when controlled, result in a direct biological response from the fish populations.\n\nLet me start by testing a hypothesis centered on the environment. If I choose climate change and habitat degradation, I am suggesting that these are the primary factors to address. While climate change is a massive driver of biodiversity loss, is it within the scope of fisheries management? A manager cannot change the global temperature, though they can create marine protected areas to help species adapt. However, the problem asks for factors that, if addressed, could reverse declines. If a stock is declining due to warming waters, a manager cannot reverse the decline by managing fishing pressure alone. Therefore, focusing on climate change might be a category error because it is an exogenous force. I will set this aside.\n\nWhat about pollution and poor management? This seems more promising. Poor management is a tautology in a way—it is the absence of the very thing we are discussing. If management is poor, the stocks decline. If management is effective, the stocks recover. This is a mechanism. Pollution, specifically chemical and plastic pollution, affects the health and reproductive success of fish. Reducing pollution through better waste management and monitoring is a concrete action a fisheries body can take. This path seems strong because it addresses both the human procedural failure and the physical environmental impact.\n\nLet me pivot to a different direction. Could the issue be primarily biological? For example, eutrophication and ocean acidification. Let me test this. Eutrophication is the process where excessive nutrients cause algal blooms, which can deplete oxygen in the water. Ocean acidification is the result of CO2 absorption by the oceans. Are these manageable through fisheries management? Fisheries management typically focuses on the harvest of mobile species. While you can manage the bycatch of plankton or the nutrients they produce, the primary drivers of eutrophication are usually agricultural runoff and land-based industrial waste. These are managed by coastal zone management or agricultural runoff regulations, not necessarily by the fisheries themselves. If I select these, I am including factors that are largely outside the direct control of the fishery's regulatory body. This contradicts the requirement to stay within the scope of fisheries management. I will reject this path.\n\nNow, let's look at the most direct mechanism: the harvest itself. If the problem is overfishing and fishing practices, does this satisfy the requirement? Overfishing is the direct result of removing too many individuals from the ecosystem. Fishing practices, such as the use of destructive gear, can physically destroy habitat or cause high levels of bycatch. Management can directly set the limits on how many fish are caught and what gear is used. This is a direct causal link: improper harvest leads to stock decline; improved management of that harvest leads to stock recovery. \n\nI should reconsider the pollution angle. Is pollution a management issue or a separate environmental issue? If I look at the Sustainable Development Goals Report 2024, it often distinguishes between the health of the ocean's chemistry and the health of its fisheries. If the decline is caused by acidification, the fishery cannot recover until the acidification is addressed. But if the decline is caused by too many boats catching too many fish, the fishery can recover immediately by simply reducing the catch. This distinction is vital. The question asks which factors, if addressed, could reverse declines. If the factor is an external chemical change, reversing it is not a matter of fisheries management. If the factor is the fishing activity itself, it is.\n\nLet me test a hybrid hypothesis. What if the decline is caused by a combination of poor management and pollution? This seems like a robust middle ground. Poor management allows for the excessive removal of biomass (overfishing) and the use of gear that damages the seafloor (fishing practices). Simultaneously, pollution degrades the water quality and the ability of the remaining fish to survive. A comprehensive management strategy would address both the how-much ( quotas) and the how (gear rules) of fishing, while also implementing protocols to reduce chemical and plastic inputs. This addresses the biological pressure (fishing) and the environmental pressure (pollution) that are within the scope of a fisheries agency.\n\nWait, I must be careful not to conflate the symptoms with the causes. Is eutrophication a cause of the decline or a symptom of a larger systemic failure? If the decline is the symptom, the causes are the human activities that drive it. The question asks for the factors that, if addressed, could reverse the decline. If I address the fishing, I address the direct removal of the population. If I address the pollution, I address the toxicity of the environment. If I address the management, I address the systemic failure to regulate the first two.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the \"C\" option regarding eutrophication and acidification. If I choose this, I am suggesting that the primary drivers of fish stock declines are chemical imbalances. But if the primary driver is the over-extraction of the resource, then managing the chemical balance does nothing to solve the overfishing problem. The chemical factors are necessary to maintain a healthy ocean, but they are not the primary drivers of fish stock collapses in a management context. A fish stock can collapse due to overfishing even in a relatively chemically stable environment. Therefore, the most direct and effective factors to address through management are the ones that control the population dynamics of the fish themselves.\n\nI will now test the strength of the \"B\" and \"D\" options against each other. Option B combines pollution and poor management. Option D focuses on fishing practices and overfishing. If I combine them, I get a more complete picture: poor management leads to overfishing and improper gear use, while poor management also fails to prevent the pollution that degrades the habitat. This creates a feedback loop. However, I must choose the single best option or a combination that fits the constraints. \n\nIf I look at the distinction between the actors and the mechanisms, the actor is the fisheries management body. The mechanisms are the regulations on catch and gear. The outputs are the health of the fish stocks. The most direct link between the actor and the output is the regulation of the fishing activity. The most direct link between the actor and the environment is the regulation of pollution. Therefore, the strongest direction must integrate the biological/extractive pressure (fishing) with the environmental/procedural pressure (pollution and management). I need to decide if \"management\" is a redundant term when paired with \"fishing practices\" and \"overfishing.\" If I include \"poor management,\" I am addressing the systemic failure. If I include \"fishing practices,\" I am addressing the specific tool used for extraction. These are distinct. One is a failure of the system, the other is the method of the activity. Both must be addressed to reverse a decline. The inclusion of pollution provides the necessary environmental component that management must also oversee to ensure stock recovery. The strongest path involves a combination of these specific, manageable factors.\n**Final Answer**"
