requirements starting from 2023. This is a plausible hypothesis. But wait, if Japan started in 2023, that means they are already in the implementation phase, whereas India is still in the preparatory or delay phase. This brings me back to the idea of immediate versus delayed disclosure. If Japan requires certain things immediately upon rule enactment, that is a much more fundamental difference in regulatory philosophy than a simple three-year delay in a full compliance date. \n\nLet's test the materiality aspect. Option D introduces the idea of non-material strategy disclosures. It says Japan allows them to be omitted indefinitely, while India requires them from 2027-2028. This is an interesting distinction regarding the quality and rigor of the data, but is it a \"timeline\" difference? Strictly speaking, a timeline is a sequence of events over time. The ability to omit something indefinitely is a temporal rule, but it isn't a \"staggered implementation\" in the same way a phased rollout is. It is more of a qualitative threshold difference. I suspect the strongest direction involves the immediate impact of the Japanese regulations compared to the deferred impact of the Indian regulations.\n\nLet's re-examine the relationship between the actors and the timing. If I focus on the immediate vs. delayed model, I can see a clear causal chain. In the Japanese model, the trigger for disclosure is the enactment of the rule itself, specifically targeting governance and risk management. In the Indian model, the trigger is effectively decoupled from the rule enactment, with a specific window of delay opening in 2025-2026. This creates two distinct states: an immediate-action state in Japan and a deferred-action state in India. This distinction covers both the timing of the start and the nature of the initial requirements. I need to verify if this \"immediate\" start in Japan specifically refers to governance and risk management, as that would differentiate it from a full-scale