ts a comprehensive overhaul of the auditing standards within the jurisdiction's regulatory framework.\n\nI am now focusing on the distinction between the amendment's effective date and the fiscal year the requirement becomes mandatory. If the amendment was made in 2023, it would set the stage for the 2024 fiscal year. This would mean the transition is already beginning. The question asks for the earliest year the transition could fully occur. If the transition is phased, the \"full\" occurrence happens when the final segment of the phase-in is reached. \n\nI will test the 2024 vs. 2029 discrepancy. If 2024 is the target, the phase must be extremely narrow or already in its final stages for the largest entities. If 2029 is the target, the phase is much more gradual, perhaps expanding from large-cap companies to medium and small enterprises. Looking at the options, the 2029 year is associated with Nigeria, the 2026 year with Pakistan, and the 2025 year with Singapore. The 2024 year is associated with New Zealand. \n\nI need to verify if the New Zealand 2024 path is viable. If the requirement is \"explicitly\" created by an amendment, there must be a clear legislative link. I will look for a connection between a 2023 amendment and a 2024 fiscal year implementation. This would mean the amendment itself is the \"trigger\" mentioned in the problem. The \"all sustainability-related matters\" part is the scope. The \"limited to reasonable\" part is the level of assurance. \n\nWait, I must ensure I am not conflating the \"amendment\" with the \"requirement.\" The requirement is the mandate to provide assurance. The amendment is the legislative action that changes the law to include this mandate. The problem asks for the jurisdiction's requirements, which are defined by this amendment. \n\nLet's double-check the Singapore 2025 path. If Singapore were the answer, the transition would be for the 2025 fiscal year. Does the Singaporean framework have a specific phased rollout for