    "ref_doc": "IPCC 1.5.pdf",
    "source_text": "364 Chapter 4 Strengthening and Implementing the Global Response 4Union (EU), individuals worry more about climate change and engage more in climate actions in countries where political party elites are united rather than divided in their support for environmental issues (Sohlberg, 2017). This section discusses how to enable and encourage behaviour and lifestyle changes that strengthen implementation of 1.5°C-consistent pathways by assessing psycho-social factors related to climate action, as well as the effects and acceptability of policy approaches targeting climate actions that are consistent with 1.5°C. Box 4.5 and Box 4.6 illustrate how these have worked in practice. 4.4.3.1 Factors related to climate actions Mitigation and adaptation behaviour is affected by many factors that shape which options are feasible and considered by individuals. Besides contextual factors (see other sub-sections in Section 4.4), these include abilities and different types of motivation to engage in behaviour. Ability to engage in climate action. Individuals more often engage in adaptation (Gebrehiwot and van der Veen, 2015; Koerth et al., 2017) and mitigation behaviour (Pisano and Lubell, 2017) when they are or feel more capable to do so. Hence, it is important to enhance ability to act on climate change, which depends on income and knowledge, among other things. A higher income is related to higher CO2 emissions; higher income groups can afford more carbon-intensive lifestyles (Lamb et al., 2014; Dietz et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2015). Yet low-income groups may lack resources to invest in energy-efficient technology and refurbishments (Andrews-Speed and Ma, 2016) and adaptation options (Wamsler, 2007; Fleming et al., 2015b; Takahashi et al., 2016). Adaptive capacity further depends on gender roles (Jabeen, 2014; Bunce and Ford, 2015), technical capacities and knowledge (Feola et al., 2015; Eakin et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2016b). Knowledge of the causes and consequences of climate change and of ways to reduce GHG emissions is not always accurate (Bord et al., 2000; Whitmarsh et al., 2011; Tobler et al., 2012), which can inhibit climate actions, even when people would be motivated to act. For example, people overestimate savings from low-energy activities, and underestimate savings from high-energy activities (Attari et al., 2010). They know little about ‘embodied’ energy (i.e., energy needed to produce products; Tobler et al., 2011), including meat (de Boer et al., 2016b). Some people mistake weather for climate (Reynolds et al., 2010), or conflate climate risks with other hazards, which can inhibit adequate adaptation (Taylor et al., 2014). More knowledge on adaptation is related to higher engagement in adaptation actions in some circumstances (Bates et al., 2009; van Kasteren, 2014; Hagen et al., 2016). How adaptation is framed in the media can influence the types of options viewed as important in different contexts (Boykoff et al., 2013; Moser, 2014; Ford and King, 2015). Knowledge is important, but is often not sufficient to motivate action (Trenberth et al., 2016). Climate change knowledge and perceptions are not strongly related to mitigation actions (Hornsey et al., 2016). Direct experience of events related to climate change influences climate concerns and actions (Blennow et al., 2012; Taylor et al., 2014), more so than second-hand information (Spence et al., 2011; Myers et al., 2012; Demski et al., 2017); high impact events with low frequency are remembered more than low impact regular events (Meze-Hausken, 2004; Singh et al., 2016b; Sullivan-Wiley and Short Gianotti, 2017). Personal experience with climate hazards strengthens motivation to protect oneself (Jabeen, 2014) and enhances adaptation actions (Bryan et al., 2009; Berrang-Ford et al., 2011; Demski et al., 2017), although this does not always translate into proactive adaptation (Taylor et al., 2014). Collectively constructed notions of risk and expectations of future climate variability shape risk perception and adaptation behaviour (Singh et al., 2016b). People with particular political views and those who emphasize individual autonomy may reject climate science knowledge and believe that there is widespread scientific disagreement about climate change (Kahan, 2010; O’Neill et al., 2013), inhibiting support for climate policy (Ding et al., 2011; McCright et al., 2013). This may explain why extreme weather experiences enhances preparedness to reduce energy use amon",
    "new_id": 549,
    "attempt": "A",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying a single factor that satisfies four distinct and complex criteria: it must be necessary for climate action, it must be a prerequisite for investing in energy-efficient technology, it must be a variable that is socially partitioned by gender roles, and it must be a variable that is personally modulated by the direct experience of climate-related events. I must first clarify the scope of the terms. Climate action in an ESG context usually refers to the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions or the adaptation to changing climates. Energy-efficient technology represents a specific mechanism of mitigation. Gender roles refer to the social and cultural distributions of power and agency. Direct experience of climate-related events implies a subjective, lived encounter with environmental shifts, rather than just abstract information. A correct solution must explain how one single concept can bridge these disparate domains.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis that the factor is purely economic, specifically higher income. If we follow this path, higher income allows for the purchase of energy-efficient technology, which satisfies the technological requirement. It also has a clear link to climate action through consumer choice. However, does higher income satisfy the gender and experience constraints? While wealth can be influenced by gendered economic structures, income itself is a metric of wealth, not a capacity for action. Furthermore, the IPCC report often discusses how wealth can actually insulate people from the direct experience of climate hazards, meaning that higher income might actually act as a barrier to the \"direct experience\" requirement rather than being linked to it. Therefore, a purely economic hypothesis is insufficient because it fails the experiential and social linkages required by the prompt.\n\nAnother path is to focus on political factors, such as political unity among elites. If elites unite, they can drive policy, which facilitates climate action and the deployment of technology. This satisfies the systemic requirement. However, does political unity among elites address the individual experience of climate events or gender roles? Typically, elite politics operates at a macro-level that ignores the granular reality of how gender roles dictate individual adaptive capacities or how a person's direct experience with a flood influences their political participation. This path is rejected because it lacks the micro-level human agency required by the question.\n\nWhat about knowledge of climate change causes? This is a common variable in ESG analysis. Knowledge drives mitigation actions, which satisfies the action requirement. It is also a prerequisite for understanding the need for energy-efficient technology. Does knowledge fit the gender and experience criteria? While knowledge can be distributed unevenly by gender, the prompt specifically mentions \"direct experience of climate-related events.\" Scientific knowledge is often abstract and global, whereas the prompt requires a link to direct, personal, and localized experience. A person can be highly knowledgeable about climate science without ever having experienced a climate-related event personally, or they can have direct experience without scientific knowledge. Because the prompt requires a link between all these things, knowledge alone is an incomplete bridge.\n\nI need to reconsider the relationship between the ability to act and the other variables. If I define the central factor as the \"ability to act,\" I can test if it serves as the common denominator. The ability to act is a capacity. This capacity is \"necessary for enhancing climate action\" because without the ability to implement changes, intentions remain unfulfilled. It is \"explicitly linked to the ability to invest in energy-efficient technology\" because the capacity to act includes the agency to make purchasing and behavioral decisions regarding technology. Now, I must test the social constraints. The prompt requires that this ability is \"influenced by gender roles.\" This is a plausible link because gender roles often dictate access to resources, decision-making power within households, and the ability to participate in economic or political markets that facilitate technological investment. Finally, I must test the experiential link. If the ability to act is \"directly experience of climate-related events,\" then the prompt's logic is that these events act as a catalyst or a trigger that enhances or alters the capacity to act. This creates a feedback loop where experience modifies the capacity, which then influences the ability to invest in technology.\n\nI must be careful not to conflate \"ability to act\" with \"willingness to act.\" The prompt asks for a factor that is \"necessary for enhancing climate action,\" which implies the capacity for action. I should also distinguish between the \"ability to act\" and \"knowledge.\" While knowledge is a component of capacity, capacity is a broader umbrella that includes economic, social, and physical capabilities. The strongest direction for a solution is to identify the central concept as a capacity-based variable that is simultaneously an economic output, a social result of gendered structures, and a psychological consequence of environmental exposure. This approach allows for a unified explanation that addresses the technological, social, and experiential requirements of the prompt.\n**Final Answer**"
