or it, but that would change the fundamental nature of the net price. Furthermore, if the standard required inflation indexing for recall disclosures, it would imply that the \"revenue\" mentioned in the recall context is not the same as the \"net price\" revenue. The most robust path is to look for a distinction where net price is a specific, deduction-based figure for the current period, while recall reporting uses a broader, historical revenue window to assess impact. This avoids the logical error of making the two metrics mathematically identical. I will focus on the tension between the immediate, deduction-excluding nature of the net price and the retrospective, impact-assessing nature of the recall revenue disclosure.\n**Final Answer**"
