ia is at the bottom of the global index but is numerically closer to the Eastern Europe/Central Asia group than to the OECD group, this inference holds. This would mean that while Nigeria is a laggard globally, its performance is not an outlier within certain broader regional groupings. This is a more sophisticated way to view performance than a simple binary of good or bad.\n\nLet's consider Option C. It states Nigeria is 146/167 and has significant challenges, but its scores exceed the worst-performing regions. I need to identify the worst-performing regions to verify this. If the data identifies specific regions as the lowest performers and Nigeria's composite score is higher than those regions, then this is a valid observation. This option balances the reality of Nigeria's low global rank with a comparative silver lining. It avoids the trap of saying Nigeria is performing well, while acknowledging that the performance is still poor by global standards.\n\nOption D is a potential pitfall. It claims Nigeria shows no improvement in any SDG. To prove this, one would have to analyze the trend data for every single one of the 17 SDGs. If the report provides a dashboard, a single counterexample—a trend showing a rising indicator—would invalidate this. Furthermore, if the data shows that Nigeria's current scores are higher than the worst-performing regions, then the claim that it is only stagnating or worsening is factually incorrect. There is a distinction between failing to meet a goal and failing to improve. If an indicator is moving, even if it is moving toward a failure, it is not stagnant. I will reject this as it is likely a hyperbolic interpretation of poor performance.\n\nI am now weighing the relationship between Options B and C. Option B provides a comparative positioning relative to specific regional and global groups, while Option C provides the specific quantitative context of the ranking and the comparison to the lowest performers. A robust inference should ideally integrate these. If I choose B, I am focusing on the relative distance to different benchmarks. If I choose C, I am focusing on the absolute position and the comparison to the tail end of the global distribution. \n\nThe most rigorous approach is to find the intersection where the comparative analysis (B) and the absolute ranking (C) are both supported by the data. I must check if the data allows for the distinction that Nigeria is a low-performing global actor (146/167) that is nevertheless closer in statistical performance to certain non-OECD regions than to the global leaders. If the data confirms that the \"worst-performing regions\" are indeed those with the lowest composite scores and that Nigeria's score is higher than those, then the strongest direction for the solution is a synthesis of these two paths: identifying the specific global percentile, acknowledging the challenges, and using the comparative data to frame the performance relative to both high-performing and low-performing global segments. This avoids the error of absolute optimism or pessimism and relies on the nuanced relationship between the data points.\n**Final Answer**"
