hat justifies the conclusion that inequality is being reduced by import levels. I must also avoid the trap of Option B, which focuses too heavily on a single dimension and uses language like \"primarily hindered\" which lacks evidence.\n\nThe strongest direction involves a nuanced synthesis that identifies specific achievements—like water access and internet availability—and specific data gaps or challenges—like urban slums, deforestation, biodiversity, and untracked R&D. I will focus on testing whether this synthesis can be structured into a single, logically consistent conclusion that addresses the social, economic, and environmental dimensions without making unsupported claims about causality or priority.\n**Final Answer**"
