    "ref_doc": "IPCC SROCC.pdf",
    "source_text": "Chapter 6 Extremes, Abrupt Changes and Managing Risks6 6 614of the global ocean area (Cheng et al. 2017; Makarim et al. 2019). The tropical Indian Ocean SST has warmed by 1.04ºC during 1950–2015, while the tropical SST warming is 0.83ºC and the global SST warning is 0.65ºC. More than 90% of the surface warming in the Indian Ocean has been attributed to changes in GHG emissions (Dong et al. 2014), with the heat redistributed in the basin via local ocean and atmospheric dynamics (Liu et al. 2015b), the ITF (Section 6.6.1; Susanto et al. 2012; Sprintall and Revelard, 2014; Lee et al. 2015b; Susanto and Song, 2015; Zhang et al. 2018) and the Walker circulation (Roxy et al. 2014; Abish et al. 2018). The dynamic processes related to the projected changes in IOD under global warming have a large inter-model spread (Cai et al. 2013). The frequency of extreme positive IOD events are projected to increase by almost a factor of three, from a one-in-seventeen-year event in the 20th century to a one-in-six-year event in the 21st century ( low confidence ). The bias in the CMIP5 models and internal variability could enlarge the projected increase in the extreme positive IOD events (Li et al. 2016a; Hui and Zheng, 2018). The increase in IOD events is not linked to the change in the frequency of El Niño events but instead to mean state change—with weakening of both equatorial westerly winds and eastward oceanic currents in association with a faster warming in the western than the eastern equatorial Indian Ocean (Cai et al. 2014b). A combination of extreme ENSO and IOD events has led to a northward shift in the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ) during 1979–2015, which is expected to increase further in the future (Freitas et al. 2017). 6.5.2 Impacts on Human and Natural Systems Increasing frequency of extreme ENSO and IOD events have the potential to have widespread impacts on natural and human systems in many parts of the globe. Though the occurrence of the extreme 2015–2016 El Niño has produced a large body of literature, it is still not clear how climate change may have altered such an impact, nor how such impacts might change in the future with increasing frequency of extreme ENSO events. We highlight here some studies that have attempted to assess the joint impact of mean change and variability. In addition to observed high variability of rainfall, severe weather events and impacts on TCs activity (Yonekura and Hall, 2014; Zhang and Guan, 2014; Wang and Liu, 2016; Zhan, 2017), extreme El Nino events have substantial impacts on natural systems which include those on marine ecosystems (Sanseverino et al. 2016; Mogollon and Calil, 2017; Ohman et al. 2017), such as severe and repeated bleaching of corals (Hughes et al. 2017a; Hughes et al. 2017b; Eakin et al. 2018), and glacial growth and retreat (Thompson et al. 2017). On the other hand, impacts on human, including managed systems are: increased incidences of forest fires (Christidis et al. 2018b; Tett et al. 2018), degraded air quality (Koplitz et al. 2015; Chang et al. 2016; Zhai et al. 2016) such as the dense haze over most parts of Indonesia and the neighboring countries in Southeast Asia as a result of prolonged Indonesian wildfires, thus imposing adverse impacts on public health in the affected areas (Koplitz et al. 2015; WMO, 2016), decreased agricultural yields in many parts of the globe (e.g., in most of the Pacific Islands countries, Thailand, eastern and southern Africa and others which resulted food insecurity, particularly in eastern and southern Africa (UNSCAP , 2015; WMO, 2016; Christidis et al. 2018b; Funk et al. 2018), and regional uptick in the number of reported cases of plague and hantavirus in Colorado and New Mexico, cholera in Tanzania, dengue in Brazil and Southeast Asia (Anyamba et al. 2019) and Zika virus in South America (Caminade et al. 2017), including increases in heat stroke cases (Christidis et al. 2018b). Substantial economic losses had resulted from droughts and floods across various parts of the globe due to teleconnections. For instance, direct losses of 10 billion USD (Sun and Miao, 2018; Yuan et al. 2018) and 6.5 billion USD (Christidis et al. 2018b) were estimated to have been incurred from severe urban inundation in cities along the Yangtze River in China and the extreme drought in Thailand, respectively. ENSO events affect TCs activity through variations in the low-level wind anomalies, vertical wind shear, mid-level relative humidity, s",
    "new_id": 610,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying the specific reason why the projected increase in extreme positive Indian Ocean Dipole events might be an overestimation. I need to clarify what is being asked. The question specifically points to a source, The Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate, which is a Special Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change. This implies that the answer must be grounded in the uncertainties and methodological limitations discussed within that specific scientific context rather than general climate theory. An accurate solution must explain the mechanism or the source of the error in the projections. I must distinguish between the physical drivers of the IOD, the models used to simulate them, and the statistical noise inherent in climate data.\n\nLet's start by testing a physical hypothesis. Perhaps the overestimation is caused by a failure in the models to accurately represent the thermodynamics of the Indian Ocean. If the models incorrectly assume that the eastern equatorial Indian Ocean is warming at the same rate as the western region, they might misrepresent the temperature gradient that drives the IOD. If the eastern ocean warms faster, it could actually suppress the positive phase of the IOD by reducing the sea surface temperature contrast. This would mean the models are overestimating the frequency of extreme positive events because they are incorrectly assuming a stronger temperature gradient than actually exists. This path focuses on the spatial distribution of warming. However, I must consider if this is a model bias issue or a process representation issue. If the models are simply missing a key physical process, like the interaction between wind and ocean currents, then the overestimation is a failure of the model's physics. But if the models are using the correct physics but are still producing too many extreme events, the problem shifts from the physical representation to the statistical reliability of the ensemble.\n\nLet's pivot to a different path. What if the overestimation is not about the models being wrong about the physics, but about the models being too similar to each other? If all the models used in the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 5 (CMIP5) are essentially using the same mathematical framework and parameterizations, they might all be making the same type of error. This would lead to a narrow inter-model spread. If the spread is small, it gives the illusion of high confidence, but if the entire ensemble is biased in the same direction, the projection is an overestimation. This would mean the overestimation is a result of a systematic error across the modeling suite. This is a different mechanism than a single model being inaccurate; it is a collective failure of the ensemble to capture the true range of possible outcomes.\n\nI should also consider the role of internal variability. Climate is not just a deterministic response to rising greenhouse gas concentrations; it is also driven by natural, chaotic fluctuations. If the models do not adequately account for these internal fluctuations, they might mistake a temporary spike in positive IOD events for a long-term trend. This would mean the overestimation is not a failure of the models' ability to represent the climate system, but a failure to distinguish between internal noise and external forcing. This path suggests that the projections are being skewed by the inherent unpredictability of the climate system itself.\n\nLet's test a third path: the role of equatorial winds. The IOD is driven by the movement of warm water across the Indian Ocean, which is facilitated by easterly winds. If the models project a weakening of these equatorial westerly winds, it should logically lead to a decrease in the positive IOD frequency. If the models are showing an increase in extreme positive events despite this weakening wind trend, there must be a disconnect. This suggests that the models might be overestimating the impact of the wind weakening or underestimating another factor that compensates for it. This leads me back to the idea of model bias. If the models are biased in how they handle the wind-current interactions, they will produce incorrect frequency projections.\n\nI need to evaluate these paths. The first path focused on the spatial warming gradient. The second path focused on model consensus and internal variability. The third path focused on wind-driven ocean currents. I must check for contradictions. If I argue that the models are overestimating due to a failure in representing the eastern warming, I am suggesting a specific physical error. But if I argue it is due to the inter-model spread being too small, I am suggesting a statistical and structural error in the ensemble. These are not mutually exclusive, but they represent different levels of the problem. The question asks for the best explanation. A strong explanation should address why the specific projections in the IPCC report might be unreliable.\n\nIf I look at the relationship between the models and the actual climate, the most robust way to explain an overestimation is to look at the discrepancy between the model ensemble and the observed reality. If the models show a high frequency of extreme events but the actual climate data does not show a corresponding increase, the models are overestimating. This discrepancy can be caused by two things: either the models are incorrectly simulating the physical mechanisms (the wind-current interactions or the temperature gradients), or the models are suffering from a lack of diversity in their projections, meaning they are all sharing a common bias. If the inter-model spread is small, it suggests that the uncertainty in the projections is being underestimated, which is a classic way to overestimate the likelihood of a specific outcome.\n\nLet's refine this. Is the overestimation a result of the models being too similar (CMIP5 bias and internal variability) or is it a result of the models failing to represent the physical reality (wind weakening and ocean currents)? If the models are all part of the same CMIP5 suite, they are likely sharing similar methodologies. If these models show a high frequency of extreme events, and this frequency is higher than what is observed or what is physically supported by the weakening wind trend, then the overestimation is a combination of a systematic model bias and the masking effect of internal variability. The small inter-model spread would then be a indicator that the uncertainty in these projections is actually much higher than the models suggest. This provides a comprehensive link between the physical drivers, the modeling framework, and the statistical output. The strongest direction involves connecting the systematic error in the model ensemble (CMIP5) with the physical inconsistency regarding the weakening winds, ultimately pointing to the underestimation of uncertainty due to the narrow spread.\n**Final Answer**"
