    "ref_page": "22",
    "ref_doc": "SASB Software & IT Services.pdf",
    "source_text": "TC-SI-330a.3. Percentage of (1) gender and (2) diversity group representation for (a) executive management, (b) non-executive management, (c) technical employees, and (d) all other employees 1The entity shall disclose (1) the percentage of gender representation among its employees for (a) executive management, (b) non-executive management, (c) technical employees and (d) all other employees. 1.1 The entity shall categorise the gender of its employees as women, men or not disclosed. 1.1.1 The entity may disclose additional categories of gender identity or expression. 1.2 The entity shall use these employee categories: (a) executive management, (b) non-executive management, (c) technical employees and (d) all other employees. 1.3 Executive management is defined as chief executives and senior officials who formulate and review the entity’s policies, and plan, direct, coordinate and evaluate the overall activities of the entity with the support of other managers. 1.3.1 The entity may refer to the International Standard Classification of Occupations (ISCO) Sub-Major Group 11 or an applicable jurisdictional occupation classification system for a definition of executive management. In such cases, the entity shall disclose the occupation classification standard used to classify executive management. 1.4 Non-executive management is defined as those who plan, direct, coordinate and evaluate the activities of the entity, or of organisational units within it, and formulate and review its policies, rules and regulations, other than executive management. 1.4.1 The entity may refer to the ISCO Major Group 1 (excluding Sub-Major Group 11) or an applicable jurisdictional occupational classification system for a definition of non-executive management. In such cases, the entity shall disclose the occupation classification standard used to classify non- executive management. 1.5 Technical employees are defined as employees who perform highly skilled or highly qualified work generally categorised in the computing, mathematical, architectural, and engineering occupations. 1.5.1 The entity may refer to the ISCO Sub-Major Groups 21 and 25 or an applicable jurisdictional occupation classification system for a definition of technical employees. In such cases, the entity shall disclose the occupation classification system used to classify technical employees. 1.6 All other employees are defined as those employees who are not classified as executive management, non- executive management or technical employees. 1.7 The entity shall calculate the percentage of gender representation for each employee category as the number of employees in each gender category divided by the total number of employees in the respective employee category. 2The entity shall disclose (2) the percentage of diversity group representation among its employees for (a) executive management, (b) non-executive management, (c) technical staff and (d) all other employees. SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |SOFTWARE & IT SERVICES |22",
    "new_id": 801,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in determining which specific deviation constitutes a violation of the requirements for disclosing gender representation under the identifier TC-SI-330a.3 within the Software & IT Services Sustainability Accounting Standard. I must first clarify what a violation of disclosure requirements implies in an ESG context. It could mean a failure to provide any data, a failure to provide the correct categories, a failure to use the correct methodology, or a failure to meet the specific granularity required by the standard. The term executive management is central, and I need to determine if the standard defines this term or if it is an ambiguous label. A correct solution must identify the specific action or omission that fails to meet the standard's specifications for this particular metric.\n\nLet's test Option A. If the entity uses ISCO Sub-Major Group 11 to define executive management but fails to state the classification standard used, is this a violation? In ESG reporting, transparency regarding the methodology is a pillar of reliability. If a reader cannot verify the definition of executive management, the comparability of the data is lost. However, I must consider if the standard specifically demands the citation of the ISCO code or if it simply demands the definition of the term. If the standard only requires defining the term, then failing to mention ISCO might be a deficiency in transparency, but is it a formal violation of the disclosure requirement for gender representation itself, or merely a violation of a best practice for documentation? If the definition is clear elsewhere in the report, the impact on the gender data's integrity might be negligible. I will set this aside as a potential documentation issue rather than a direct violation of the gender disclosure rule.\n\nNow, let's look at Option B. The entity uses a three-part categorization: women, men, and not disclosed, with a clarifying footnote. Does this violate the requirement? Most ESG standards for gender representation require reporting on the proportion of women in specific roles. If the entity provides the three categories and explains the 'not disclosed' bucket, they are essentially providing a complete picture of their data's coverage. The footnote actually enhances the quality of the disclosure by addressing the 'not disclosed' value, which is a common source of ambiguity in ESG data. This seems to be a robust way to handle the data, so a violation here is unlikely unless the standard strictly mandates a binary classification without a 'not disclosed' option. I will reject this as a violation because the explanation provided actually mitigates the risk of misinterpretation.\n\nLet's move to Option C. The entity calculates the gender representation percentage for technical employees by excluding those who selected 'not disclosed' from the total count. I need to examine the mathematical mechanism here. If the numerator represents the number of women or men in a specific role, and the denominator is the total number of people in that role, what happens when some are 'not disclosed'? If the 'not disclosed' individuals are simply omitted from the denominator, the resulting percentage becomes a measure of the disclosed population rather than the actual population. This creates a selection bias where the reported representation appears more stable or extreme than it actually is. If the standard requires the gender representation of the total workforce or a specific role, then the denominator must represent the entire group, including those whose gender is not disclosed. Excluding the 'not disclosed' category from the total count changes the fundamental metric from role-based representation to a subset-based representation. This appears to be a direct mathematical violation of the requirement to report on the gender composition of a defined group.\n\nI should reconsider Option D. The entity combines executive and non-executive management into a single group. Does this violate the requirement for gender representation? If the standard requires separate reporting for executive management, then combining these groups would violate the granularity requirement. However, if the standard only requires the disclosure of gender representation for the entity as a whole and the combination does not obscure the required data, it might not be a violation. But wait, if the standard specifically uses the term executive management, there is an implicit expectation of distinction. If I combine all management, I am effectively masking the gender composition of the executive tier. This would be a violation of the requirement to disclose the specific group's representation.\n\nI must weigh the mathematical error in Option C against the categorical error in Option D. The mathematical error in Option C is a fundamental failure of the calculation's integrity, as it alters the denominator to exclude unknowns, which is a statistical no-no in demographic reporting. The categorical error in Option D is a failure to provide the specific granularity required by the standard. While both are errors, the mathematical error in Option C results in a false representation of the ratio itself, whereas the categorical error in Option D results in a loss of specific group-level data. In the context of ESG auditing, a failure in the mathematical mechanism—the way the percentage is derived—is a more fundamental violation of the disclosure's accuracy than a failure in the organizational categorization, which is more of a descriptive error. I will focus on the distinction between the numerator, which represents the specific gender counts, and the denominator, which represents the total population including those with unknown gender. The strongest direction for the solution is to identify the specific error in the relationship between the disclosed counts and the total population count.\n**Final Answer**"
