    "source_text": "Tagging Figure E1 using the elements in Table E1 would result in the data in Table E2. Table E2—Illustration of tagged information Element Water quality regulation [member]Flooding [member] Value Description of risk or opportunity (IFRS S1.30(a), IFRS S2.10(a))Water quality regulation Stricter water quality regulation might require us to replace some equipment used in our water treatment plants.Flooding Flooding might disrupt the availa- bility of our water treatment plants. We identify flooding as a climate- related physical risk. Topic(s) of risk or opportunity (IFRS S1.30(a), IFRS S2.10(a))- Climate topic Category of climate-related risk (IFRS S2.10(b))- Physical risk Time horizon(s) over which effects of risk or opportunity could reasonably be expected to occur (IFRS S1.30(b), IFRS S2.10(c))We expect the effect of this risk to occur in the short term.We expect the effect of this risk to occur in the short term, growing in severity in the medium term. Time horizon(s) over which effects of risk or opportunity could reasonably be expected to occur (IFRS S1.30(b), IFRS S2.10(c))Short term Short term Medium term Current and anticipated effects of risks and opportunities on business model and value chain (IFRS S1.32(a), IFRS S2.13(a))We assess that water quality regulation risk can affect particu- lar parts of our business model because ....We assess that disruption caused by flooding can affect particular parts of our business model because .... As illustrated in Table E2, a dimensional taxonomy structure allows users of general purpose financial reports to extract information for each risk and opportunity (for example, information related to water quality regulation separately from information related to flooding) in a digital format. However, risks and opportunities are defined by an entity (and tagged using entity-specific elements) which might affect users of general purpose financial reports’ efficient analysis and comparison of this information between entities. As discussed in paragraph E1, the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Taxonomy includes elements for tagging information about sustainability-related risks and opportunities described as relating to a sustainability-related topic (or topics), for example, a climate-related topic. As illustrated in Table E2 , those elements allow users of general purpose financial reports to extract in a digital format information about: (a) which topics the sustainability-related risks and opportunities relate to, which helps users of general purpose financial reports to understand sustainability-related risks and opportunities identified by an entity. For example, users of general purpose financial reports would be able to identify efficiently in a digital format that the ‘Flooding’ risk identified by an entity is a climate-related risk. (b) which disclosures are related to climate and other sustainability-related topics, thus facilitating users of general purpose financial reports’ analysis of those disclosures. For example, users of general purpose financial reports will be able to identify information about current and anticipated effects of climate- related risks and opportunities on the entity’s business model and value chain applying the requirement in paragraph 13(a) of IFRS S2 based on explicit information in a digital format about which risks and opportunities relate to climate, as explained in paragraph E9(a). (c) sustainability-related topics, which could serve as a common basis for comparing sustainability-related risks and opportunities between entities. This could also help users of general purpose financial reports to compare information about entities’ climate-related risks and opportunities that meets those requirements of the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards and other sustainability-related disclosure standards that are aligned with IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards.E7 E8 E9IFRS S USTAINABILITY DISCLOSURE TAXONOMY APRIL 2024 © IFRS Foundation 44",
    "new_id": 810,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of the problem lies in the tension between specificity and comparability within the framework of the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Taxonomy. I must first clarify what is meant by entity-specific tagging. Does this refer to the use of unique identifiers for internal processes, or the application of bespoke labels to standard taxonomy terms? If it is the former, the issue is one of data silos; if it is the latter, it is an issue of semantic ambiguity. The question asks why this tagging might hinder the comparison of disclosures between entities. A correct solution must explain the mechanism by which specificity becomes a barrier to cross-entity visibility, rather than just stating that comparisons are hard.\n\nLet's test the hypothesis that entity-specific tagging refers to the use of proprietary identifiers. If every entity uses its own internal codes for external risks, a user would indeed struggle to compare them. However, this is a problem of data integration, not necessarily a failure of the taxonomy's ability to define what is being disclosed. If the taxonomy provides a standard label for \"water scarcity,\" but the entity tags it with an internal code that doesn't map back to the standard, the comparison is lost. But is this the same as entity-specific tagging of risks and opportunities? Not quite. This is more about internal vs. external tagging. I need to focus on how the tagging of the actual content—the risks and opportunities—impacts the user.\n\nPerhaps the issue is that entity-specific tagging allows for too much nuance. If one entity tags a risk as \"transition risk due to carbon pricing\" and another as \"regulatory transition risk for steel production,\" the user sees two different tags. Is the problem that the risks are different, or that the tags are different? If the risks are different, they are incomparable by definition. But the question asks why the tagging might hinder comparison, which implies that the risks themselves might be comparable if they were tagged consistently.\n\nLet's look at the mechanism of the IFRS S1 and S2 standards. These standards aim to provide a structured way to disclose sustainability-related financial information. They rely on a taxonomy of terms to ensure that \"climate risk\" isn't just a vague concept but a specific one. If an entity adds specific, non-standardized tags to these terms—say, tagging a risk as \"our specific climate risk\" instead of \"climate risk transition\"—the user faces a cognitive load. They must now interpret whether \"our specific climate risk\" is a subset of \"climate risk\" or a completely different category. This creates a barrier to comparison because the user cannot easily map the entity's specific terminology back to the standard taxonomy used by other entities.\n\nI should consider if the problem is actually the lack of a dimensional taxonomy structure. If the IFRS framework did not support tagging risks and opportunities within the same dimension, entities might be forced to create their own hybrid structures. This would lead to a fragmented landscape where no two companies tag their risks in the same way. However, the question asks why the *entity-specific* tagging is the hindrance, not whether the taxonomy is capable of supporting a structure. This suggests the problem is the application of specificity rather than the absence of a structure.\n\nWait, I must distinguish between the content of the disclosure and the tag itself. The content is the \"what\" (the risk or opportunity), and the tag is the \"label\" applied to it. If the tag is specific to the entity's internal logic, it might fail to align with the standard's hierarchical or dimensional requirements. For example, if the taxonomy requires a risk to be tagged at a certain level of granularity, but the entity tags it at a higher or lower level to fit their specific reporting needs, the user is left to guess the intended level of the disclosure. This mismatch in granularity is a direct consequence of entity-specific tagging.\n\nIs it possible that the problem is simply that all sustainability risks are too qualitative to be compared? If I choose option C, I am arguing that the nature of the subject matter is the barrier. But that ignores the specific prompt about tagging. The question asks why the *tagging* might be the hindrance. If the risks were purely quantitative, like carbon emissions in metric tons, the comparison would be easy regardless of the tagging. The fact that we are discussing tagging implies we are dealing with the categorization of qualitative or semi-quantitative information. Therefore, the issue isn't that risks are incomparable, but that the specific way they are labeled makes them appear incomparable.\n\nLet's refine the hypothesis. The mechanism is the mismatch between the standard's predefined taxonomy terms and the entity's decision to append or modify those terms with specific, non-standard descriptors. The actor is the entity performing the tagging, and the stakeholder is the user trying to compare disclosures. The output is a set of tagged disclosures that are difficult to aggregate. If an entity tags a risk as \"transition risk specific to our aluminum smelting process,\" the user must determine if this falls under the broader \"climate transition risk\" category defined by the taxonomy. If the tagging is too specific, it obscures the alignment with the standard categories, making it difficult for a user to see the forest for the trees. This seems like a much stronger direction than the idea that the taxonomy itself lacks structure or that all risks are inherently incomparable. The focus should remain on the tension between the need for specificity in internal reporting and the need for standardization in external comparison.\n**Final Answer**"
