    "id": 77875,
    "question": "Which of the following best captures the implicit relationship between supplier engagement strategies and their role in mitigating risks related to both animal welfare and human rights, as discussed in the IFRS S2 Industry-based Guidance on Implementing Climate-related Disclosures?",
    "options": {
      "C": "Supplier contracts addressing labour and human rights issues are likely to include specific clauses related to animal welfare certifications.",
      "A": "Supplier training programs on environmental management inherently ensure compliance with animal welfare standards.",
      "B": "Diversification of suppliers is primarily aimed at addressing reputational damage caused by antibiotic use in animal protein production.",
      "D": "Supplier engagement on human rights issues indirectly supports animal welfare by fostering a culture of ethical practices across the supply chain.",
      "Z": "Not sure"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "ref_page": "225-226",
    "ref_doc": "IFRS S2 Industry.pdf",
    "source_text": "1.1.7 Animal welfare, human rights or related supply chain incidents that may result in reputational damage 1.2 Relevant strategies to discuss may include supplier screening, diversification of suppliers, supplier training programmes on best environmental management practices, supplier engagement on labour and human rights issues, and maintenance of a supply chain code of conduct, supply chain audits and certifications. 2 The entity may identify which products or product lines present risks to its operations, the risks represented, and the strategies the entity uses to mitigate such risks. 3 The entity shall discuss its animal welfare standards applicable to its supply chain. 3.1 Animal welfare standards are defined as policies for beef, pork, poultry or dairy production conditions, including: 3.1.1 Animal treatment and handling 3.1.2 Housing and transportation conditions 3.1.3 Slaughter facilities and procedures 3.1.4 Use of antibiotics and hormones 3.2 Discussion shall include, but is not limited to: 3.2.1 Any targets the entity has related to animal welfare standards and its progress toward those targets 3.2.2 Any requirements for suppliers related to animal welfare standards 3.2.3 How, if in any way, animal welfare standards are addressed in supplier contracts 4 The entity shall describe its use of animal welfare certifications. Certifications may include: Animal Welfare Approved, Certified Humane Program, Food Alliance Certified and Global Animal Partnership 5-Step Animal Welfare Rating Program. 5 The entity may disclose the percentage of animal protein sold, by animal protein type, that is produced without medically important antibiotics. 5.1 The percentage is calculated as the carcass (or dressed) weight of animal protein purchased that did not receive medically important antibiotics at any stage of its life divided by the total carcass (or dressed) weight of animal protein purchased. 224 © IFRS Foundation\n\n[Page 226]\nVolume 27 —Drug Retailers Industry Description Drug Retailers industry entities operate retail pharmacies and distribution centres that supply retail stores. Stores may be entity-owned or franchised. Large entities source drugs and other merchandise through wholesalers and distributors. Consumer sales of prescription and over-the-counter pharmaceutical products generate a majority of the industry ’s revenue; other goods sold include household goods, personal care products and a limited selection of groceries. Additionally, the pharmacy retailer segment is expanding its health-focused services by offering clinics at various retail locations, which may add to the industry ’s shifting sustainability landscape. Sustainability Disclosure Topics & Metrics Table 1. Sustainability Disclosure Topics & Metrics TOPIC METRIC CATEGORY UNIT OF MEASURECODE Energy Management in Retail(1) Total energy consumed, (2) percentage grid electricity and (3) percentage renewableQuantitative Gigajoules (GJ), Percentage (%)HC-DR-130a.1 Table 2. Activity Metrics ACTIVITY METRIC CATEGORY UNIT OF MEASURECODE Number of pharmacy locations Quantitative Number HC-DR-000.A Total area of retail spaceQuantitative Square metres (m²)HC-DR-000.B Number of prescriptions filled, percentage for controlled substancesQuantitative Number, Percentage (%)HC-DR-000.C Number of pharmacists 28Quantitative Number HC-DR-000.D Energy Management in Retail Topic Summary Chain drug retailers operate thousands of locations that consume large quantities of energy. Electricity is used primarily for lighting and refrigeration. Many retail locations may operate 24 hours a day, thereby increasing energy demand. Operational energy efficiency and diversification among a range of energy supply sources may mitigate exposure to rising energy costs and limit an entity ’s indirect greenhouse gas emissions. 28Pharmacists are employees who dispense drugs prescribed by physicians and other health practitioners and provide information to patients about medications and their use. Pharmacists may advise physicians and other health practitioners on the selection, dosage, interactions, and side effects of medications.IFRS S2 CLIMATE-RELATED DISCLOSURES –JUNE 2023 © IFRS Foundation 225",
    "new_id": 863,
    "attempt": "D",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying the specific relationship between two distinct ESG pillars—human rights and animal welfare—through the mechanism of supplier engagement. I must first clarify what is being asked. The question seeks the best capture of the implicit relationship, which suggests that the correct answer must demonstrate a functional or causal link between the two topics within the context of the guidance. I need to be careful with the term engagement; it is not a monolithic concept. It can range from passive monitoring to active contractual integration. A correct solution must explain how actions taken regarding one pillar (human rights) affect or are connected to the other (animal welfare) through the actors involved in the supply chain.\n\nLet me test a first hypothesis. Perhaps the relationship is one of direct substitution. In this view, a company focuses on human rights because it is a legal requirement, and by doing so, it inadvertently covers animal welfare. If a company implements strict labor audits, it creates a more disciplined supply chain that is less likely to cut corners on animal treatment. However, I must reject this. There is no inherent causal link in the logic of corporate reporting where a human rights audit automatically triggers an animal welfare audit. They are distinct operational domains. One is about the treatment of workers, the other about the treatment of sentient non-human organisms. While they both fall under the umbrella of social responsibility, one does not functionally replace the other.\n\nLet me try a second path. Could the relationship be based on the mechanism of contractuality? If the guidance suggests that supplier engagement involves specific contractual clauses, then I should look for an option that links the content of those contracts to both topics. If a company includes human rights clauses in its contracts, does it naturally include animal welfare? I need to look at the actors here. The company is the actor, the suppliers are the targets, and the mechanism is the contract. If the contract is the vehicle, what is the output? The output would be a standardized level of ethical behavior. But does a contract for human rights labor automatically mandate animal welfare certifications? Not necessarily. A contract might focus on wage parity without mentioning livestock conditions. Therefore, the relationship must be more nuanced than simple contractual inclusion.\n\nWhat if the relationship is driven by the objective of risk mitigation? This seems more robust. The guidance is part of an ESG framework, and ESG frameworks are fundamentally about managing non-financial risks. I can categorize these risks into two types: reputational and operational. Animal welfare issues, such as cruelty or improper handling, are significant reputational risks. Human rights abuses in the supply chain are also major reputational and operational risks. If a company's engagement strategy is designed to mitigate reputational risk, then it must address all major risk vectors in the supply chain. This would mean that the strategy for human rights is part of a broader spectrum of ethical oversight that naturally extends to animal welfare.\n\nLet me test this risk-mitigation hypothesis against the diversification idea. Option B suggests that diversification of suppliers is aimed at reputational damage caused by antibiotic use. While this is a specific animal welfare issue, it feels too narrow. The question asks for the relationship between the two pillars generally, not just a specific instance of one pillar leading to the other. If I focus only on antibiotic use, I am ignoring the broader structural connection between how a company manages its social footprint and its biological footprint.\n\nLet's reconsider the contractual path, but this time through the lens of procedure rather than content. Instead of looking at what the clauses *are*, let's look at how they are *used*. If a company engages with suppliers to address human rights, it is engaging with them on a procedural level—how they manage their workforce. If it then extends that engagement to animal welfare, it is applying that same procedural oversight to a different asset class. The relationship is not that one replaces the other, but that the *method* of engagement is being applied across different domains of risk. This is a stronger direction. It moves away from the idea that \"human rights equals animal welfare\" and instead suggests that \"the method of managing human rights risks is structurally similar to and complementary with the method of managing animal welfare risks.\"\n\nWait, I must be careful not to conflate the two. They are different. A worker's right to a safe environment is not the same as an animal's right to humane treatment. However, in a corporate governance framework, they are both managed through the same mechanism: supplier engagement, contract enforcement, and risk assessment. The \"diversification\" idea in Option B is a specific tactical response to a specific risk (antibiotic resistance/reputational damage), whereas the \"contractual\" and \"engagement\" ideas in the other options describe the structural way a company manages its entire supply chain.\n\nLet's look at the distinction between the objectives. The objective of human rights engagement is to ensure labor standards and social equity. The objective of animal welfare engagement is to ensure ethical treatment and often, food safety or biological integrity. If a company's engagement strategy is \"holistic,\" then the relationship is that the strategy is a unified approach to managing the social dimension of the supply chain. This would mean that the engagement on human rights is a prerequisite for, or at least a parallel track to, engagement on animal welfare. \n\nIs there a flaw in this? If a company only focuses on human rights, is it neglecting its ESG duties regarding animal welfare? Yes, because animal welfare is a distinct material topic. Therefore, the \"best capture\" of the relationship must be something that describes how these two distinct topics are addressed through a single, integrated approach to supplier management. The strongest direction involves the idea that engagement on human rights issues sets a precedent or utilizes a framework that is then applied to the mitigation of animal welfare risks, creating a cohesive strategy for managing the social and ethical dimensions of the supply chain.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
