    "id": 78062,
    "question": "When calculating the percentage of wood-fibre-based materials certified to multiple third-party certifications, how should entities account for fibre that meets several standards simultaneously, according to the Pulp & Paper Products – Sustainability Accounting Standard?",
    "options": {
      "C": "They must include the full weight of such fibre in calculations for each relevant certification without dividing it.",
      "A": "They must allocate the weight of such fibre proportionally across all relevant certifications.",
      "B": "They must exclude such fibre entirely from calculations to avoid double-counting.",
      "D": "They must assign the fibre to only one certification based on a prioritization scheme.",
      "Z": "Not sure"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "ref_page": "21",
    "ref_doc": "SASB Pulp & Paper Products.pdf",
    "source_text": "1.3 The scope of wood-fibre-based materials includes all inputs processed to be sold as a finished good, including recycled raw materials, virgin raw materials, and goods consumed directly in the production process and excluding biomass for energy. 2The percentage of wood-fibre-based materials from third-party certified forestlands shall be calculated as the total weight (in air dried metric tonnes) of the entity ’s wood-fibre-based materials sourced from third-party certified forestlands divided by the total weight (in air dried metric tonnes) of wood-fibre-based materials sourced. 3The entity shall disclose the percentage of the total wood-fibre-based materials from third-party certified forestlands certified to each standard (for example, FSC Chain of Custody, PEFC Chain of Custody and SFI Chain of Custody). 3.1 The entity shall calculate the percentage of wood-fibre-based materials certified to each standard as the amount of wood-fibre-based materials third-party certified to the respective standard divided by the total amount of wood fibre sourced by the entity. 3.2 If wood-fibre is certified to multiple third-party certifications, the entity shall include the amount of such fibre in its calculations for each relevant certification. 4The entity shall disclose the percentage of its total wood-fibre-based materials sourced from non-third-party certified forestlands but meets other fibre sourcing standards, including: 4.1 Responsible fibre sourcing standards (for example, SFI Fibre Sourcing Standard) 4.2 Controlled wood standards (for example, FSC Controlled Wood Certification and PEFC Controlled Wood) 4.3 Recycled fibre standards that include post- and pre-consumer reclaimed material (for example, PEFC Controlled Sources, FSC Recycled Label and SFI Recycled Label) 4.4 Any other due diligence standards that cover sourcing requirements for fibre from non-certified forestlands 5For fibre from non-certified forestlands that meets multiple fibre sourcing standards, the entity shall not account for the weight more than once when calculating the total percentage of fibre from non-certified forestlands that meets other fibre sourcing standards. 6The entity shall disclose the percentage of wood fibre that meets each sourcing standard (for example, FSC Controlled Wood, SFI Fibre Sourcing Standard and PEFC Controlled Sources). 6.1 If wood fibre meets multiple sourcing standards, the entity shall include the amount of such fibre in its calculations for each relevant sourcing standard. Note to RR-PP-430a.1 1The entity shall discuss its due diligence practices for fibre that is not from certified forestlands or certified to other fibre sourcing standards and its policies to verify the forestry management and harvesting practices of suppliers, which may include codes of conduct, audits or contracts, among others. 2 The entity shall disclose how it verifies that its non-certified fibre includes criteria for the following: SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |PULP & PAPER PRODUCTS |21",
    "new_id": 883,
    "attempt": "A",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in the mathematical and logical treatment of overlapping data sets within an ESG reporting framework. I need to determine how a single unit of wood-fiber-based material, which satisfies the criteria for multiple third-party certifications, is represented in a percentage calculation. The objective is to find the method that accurately reflects the total volume of material certified under each specific scheme without violating the principles of the Pulp & Paper Products – Sustainability Accounting Standard. I must clarify what is meant by \"certified to multiple third-party certifications.\" This implies that a single material might meet the rigorous requirements of, for example, both the Forest Stewardship Council and the Programme for the Endorsement of Forest Certification. A correct solution must explain the mechanism for counting this shared volume in a way that prevents inflation of the total certified percentage. I will first consider the possibility that the standard requires a strict one-to-one mapping. If an entity uses Option D, where they assign the fibre to only one certification based on a prioritization scheme, they would be effectively discarding the information about the other certifications the fibre possesses. This would result in a highly conservative and potentially under-reported figure for the less prioritized certifications, which fails to capture the actual environmental attributes of the material. This seems like an unlikely solution for a standard designed to capture sustainability performance. I will then test the opposite extreme, which is Option C, where the fibre is included in full for every certification it meets. If a single piece of fibre is counted as 100% compliant with three different standards, the entity's reported percentage of certified material would be three times the actual physical volume of that fibre. This would lead to a gross distortion of the data, making the reported sustainability performance appear much higher than the actual physical reality. This is a clear violation of the standard's requirement for accuracy. I must now look at the concept of allocation. If I move toward a fractional approach, I encounter a choice between two different ways to handle the overlap. In Option A, the fibre is allocated proportionally across all relevant certifications. In Option B, the fibre is excluded entirely to avoid double-counting. Let's test the exclusion approach in Option B. If a material is certified under two standards, and we simply ignore it to keep the numbers \"clean,\" we are essentially treating the multi-certified status as a non-event. This would mean that the reported percentage of certified material would be lower than the actual amount of high-quality, multi-compliant fibre in the supply chain. This under-reporting would provide a misleadingly low indication of the company's sustainability efforts. Now, let's look at the proportional allocation in Option A. If a fibre is equally compliant with two standards, it is assigned a 50% weight to each. This method treats the certification as a property that can be distributed according to the degree of compliance. The mechanism here is to divide the weight of the fibre by the number of certifications it holds. I need to check if this avoids the pitfalls of the others. Does it prevent inflation? Yes, because the total weight assigned to all certifications combined will still equal the physical weight of the fibre. Does it prevent under-reporting? Yes, because it recognizes the full weight of the fibre rather than discarding it. The logic holds because the standard is likely designed to capture the breadth of certification across the supply chain. The most rigorous way to handle this is to treat the certifications as overlapping categories that must be normalized through division to ensure that the sum of all certified percentages reflects the true, non-duplicated physical volume of the material. This approach respects the multi-dimensional nature of the fibre's attributes while maintaining the integrity of the mathematical sum. I have rejected the exclusion and the full-inclusion methods because they lead to under-reporting and over-inflation, respectively. The most robust path is the proportional distribution of the fibre's weight across its multiple certifications.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
