    "correct_answer": "B",
    "ref_page": "8-9",
    "ref_doc": "SASB Health Care Delivery.pdf",
    "source_text": "Energy Management Topic Summary Health Care Delivery entities operate energy-intensive facilities and rely on both purchased electricity and fuel. The consumption of both can contribute to environmental impacts, including climate change and pollution. Legislative attempts to limit these impacts and to incentivise energy efficiency and renewable energy may result in price volatility associated with fossil fuels and conventional electricity. Entities that improve energy efficiency may decrease costs and limit exposure to energy price fluctuations. Metrics HC-DY-130a.1. (1) Total energy consumed, (2) percentage grid electricity and (3) percentage renewable 1 The entity shall disclose (1) the total amount of energy it consumed as an aggregate figure, in gigajoules (GJ). 1.1 The scope of energy consumption includes energy from all sources, including energy purchased from external sources and energy produced by the entity itself (self-generated). For example, direct fuel usage, purchased electricity, and heating, cooling and steam energy are all included within the scope of energy consumption. 1.2 The scope of energy consumption includes only energy directly consumed by the entity during the reporting period. 1.3 In calculating energy consumption from fuels and biofuels, the entity shall use higher heating values (HHV), also known as gross calorific values (GCV), which are measured directly or taken from the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC). 2The entity shall disclose (2) the percentage of energy it consumed that was supplied from grid electricity. 2.1 The percentage shall be calculated as purchased grid electricity consumption divided by total energy consumption. 3The entity shall disclose (3) the percentage of energy it consumed that was renewable energy. 3.1 Renewable energy is defined as energy from sources that are replenished at a rate greater than or equal to their rate of depletion, such as geothermal, wind, solar, hydro and biomass. 3.2 The percentage shall be calculated as renewable energy consumption divided by total energy consumption. 3.3 The scope of renewable energy includes renewable fuel the entity consumed, renewable energy the entity directly produced and renewable energy the entity purchased, if purchased through a renewable power purchase agreement (PPA) that explicitly includes renewable energy certificates (RECs) or Guarantees of Origin (GOs), a Green ‐e Energy Certified utility or supplier programme, or other green power products that explicitly include RECs or GOs, or for which Green ‐e Energy Certified RECs are paired with grid electricity. SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |HEALTH CARE DELIVERY |8\n\n[Page 9]\n3.3.1 For any renewable electricity generated on-site, any RECs and GOs shall be retained (not sold) and retired or cancelled on behalf of the entity for the entity to claim them as renewable energy. 3.3.2 For renewable PPAs and green power products, the agreement shall explicitly include and convey that RECs and GOs be retained or replaced and retired or cancelled on behalf of the entity for the entity to claim them as renewable energy. 3.3.3 The renewable portion of the electricity grid mix that is outside of the control or influence of the entity is excluded from the scope of renewable energy. 3.4 For the purposes of this disclosure, the scope of renewable energy from biomass sources is limited to materials certified to a third-party standard (for example, Forest Stewardship Council, Sustainable Forest Initiative, Programme for the Endorsement of Forest Certification, or American Tree Farm System), materials considered eligible sources of supply according to the Green-e Framework for Renewable Energy Certification, Version 1.0 (2017) or Green-e regional standards, and/or materials that are eligible for an applicable state renewable portfolio standard. 4The entity shall apply conversion factors consistently for all data reported under this disclosure, such as the use of HHVs for fuel usage (including biofuels) and conversion of kilowatt hours (kWh) to GJ (for energy data including electricity from solar or wind energy). SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |HEALTH CARE DELIVERY |9",
    "new_id": 914,
    "attempt": "D",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific constraints of the Health Care Delivery – Sustainability Accounting Standard regarding the disclosure of renewable energy. I must clarify what categorically exclude means in this context. It implies a total omission from the total consumption disclosure, not just a lack of separate voluntary reporting. I also need to define the key terms: RECs or GOs represent the environmental attributes of the energy, on-site generation refers to production occurring within the entity's own facilities, and a PPA is a power purchase agreement. A correct solution must identify the specific condition where the standard permits a renewable energy source to be ignored in the total consumption metric.\n\nLet's start by testing the on-site generation scenario. If an entity generates its own renewable energy on-site but sells the RECs or GOs to a third party, does it meet the exclusion condition? I need to look at the mechanism of the standard. Most ESG standards, including those for healthcare delivery, aim to capture the entity's actual renewable contribution to reduce carbon footprints. If the entity sells the attributes, it is essentially selling its ability to claim that renewable energy. If the standard requires reporting on the renewable portion of total consumption, selling the RECs would typically mean the entity must still account for the renewable energy in its consumption profile, even if it doesn't own the certificate. Therefore, Option A seems to suggest an exclusion based on a transfer of certificates, but this might actually trigger a reporting requirement under most standards. I will reject this path because selling the attributes usually prevents the entity from claiming the energy as its own, rather than allowing it to exclude it.\n\nNow, let's consider the grid mix scenario. If the renewable energy is part of the broader electricity grid mix that the entity cannot control or influence, does this allow for exclusion? This feels like a weak argument. Most ESG standards require reporting on the carbon intensity of the grid mix as a baseline. If the entity is consuming electricity from a grid that contains renewable energy, the standard usually requires disclosing the proportion of that grid's renewable content. To exclude it entirely would mean claiming the entity's consumption is entirely non-renewable, which would be a misrepresentation of the actual energy mix. This path is likely incorrect because the standard usually mandates reporting the grid's renewable share, even if the entity has no direct control over it.\n\nWhat about biomass? Option C suggests excluding renewable energy derived from biomass that lacks third-party certification or state renewable portfolio status. I need to distinguish between the source of the energy and the verification of the source. Biomass is a renewable source, but its environmental benefit is highly dependent on the lifecycle of the material. If the standard requires renewable energy to be \"verified\" or \"certified\" to ensure it provides a genuine reduction in emissions, then unverified biomass might be excluded from the \"renewable\" category. However, the question asks if the entity can categorically exclude the energy from its total consumption disclosure. Most standards require reporting total energy consumption regardless of the source's certification status; they might just require a separate disclosure of \"uncertified\" or \"non-verified\" renewables. This doesn't necessarily mean the energy can be entirely omitted from the total consumption metric. I will reject this path because the focus is usually on the total energy volume, not just the renewable attribute.\n\nThis leaves me with the PPA scenario. Let's test the condition where the renewable energy is purchased through a PPA that does not explicitly include or convey RECs or GOs. I need to analyze the relationship between the physical energy and the certificate. In a traditional PPA, the entity enters a contract to purchase energy from a specific source. If the contract is for the energy itself but does not include the renewable attributes, the entity is essentially purchasing \"green\" energy without the \"green\" certificate. In many accounting frameworks, the ability to claim a renewable energy contribution is tied to the ownership of the certificate. If the PPA does not convey the RECs or GOs, the entity lacks the formal mechanism to claim that specific energy as part of its renewable portfolio. If the standard defines renewable consumption as the amount of energy for which the entity holds the renewable attribute, then a PPA without certificates would not meet the definition of \"renewable consumption\" under that specific accounting rule. This is a crucial distinction: the energy is still being consumed, but the accounting standard's definition of \"renewable\" requires the certificate. This creates a technical gap where the energy is physically present in the consumption, but the accounting treatment allows it to be excluded from the renewable category.\n\nTo stress-test this, I must consider if any other mechanism would allow this exclusion. Could a lack of data lead to an exclusion? If the entity simply cannot measure the renewable energy, they might be forced to exclude it, but that is a data limitation, not a condition of the energy's nature. The PPA scenario is different because it is a deliberate contractual structure. The exclusion isn't due to a lack of information, but due to the specific legal and accounting definition of what constitutes a \"renewable\" purchase. The absence of the certificate in the PPA is the critical variable that prevents the energy from being categorized as renewable, even though the energy is being used. This path is the most robust because it relies on the interplay between the physical act of consumption and the legal requirement of attribute ownership, which is a common tension in ESG accounting standards.\n**Final Answer**"
