    "ref_page": "26-27",
    "ref_doc": "GRI 1_ Foundation 2021.pdf",
    "source_text": "Stakeholder or expert panels The organization can also convene a stakeholder or expert panel to seek views on its approach to sustainability reporting or for advice on the information to be reported.competent in applying quality control procedures to the assurance engagement;• able to conduct the engagement in a manner that is systematic, documented, evidence-based, and characterized by defined procedures in line with professional standards for assurance;• able to consider the selection of the information reported as well as its accuracy, and to assess whether the reporting provides a comprehensive picture of the organization’s most significant impacts and how it manages these impacts;• able to assess the extent to which the organization has applied the GRI Standards in formulating opinions or reaching conclusions.• GRI 1: Foundation 2021 26\n\n[Page 27]\nGlossary This glossary provides definitions for terms used in this Standard. The organization is required to apply these definitions when using the GRI Standards. The definitions included in this glossary may contain terms that are further defined in the complete GRI Standards Glossary . All defined terms are underlined. If a term is not defined in this glossary or in the complete GRI Standards Glossary , definitions that are commonly used and understood apply. business partner entity with which the organization has some form of direct and formal engagement for the purpose of meeting its business objectives Source: Shift and Mazars LLP, UN Guiding Principles Reporting Framework , 2015; modified Examples: affiliates, business-to-business customers, clients, first-tier suppliers , franchisees, joint venture partners, investee companies in which the organization has a shareholding position Note: Business partners do not include subsidiaries and affiliates that the organization controls. business relationships relationships that the organization has with business partners , with entities in its value chain including those beyond the first tier, and with any other entities directly linked to its operations, products, or services Source: United Nations (UN), Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights: Implementing the United Nations “Protect, Respect and Remedy” Framework , 2011; modified Note: Examples of other entities directly linked to the organization’s operations, products, or services are a non-governmental organization with which the organization delivers support to a local community or state security forces that protect the organization’s facilities. child person under the age of 15 years, or under the age of completion of compulsory schooling, whichever is higher Note 1: Exceptions can occur in certain countries where economies and educational facilities are insufficiently developed, and a minimum age of 14 years applies. These countries of exception are specified by the International Labour Organization (ILO) in response to a special application by the country concerned and in consultation with representative organizations of employers and workers. Note 2: The ILO Minimum Age Convention, 1973, (No. 138), refers to both child labor and young workers. due diligence process to identify, prevent, mitigate , and account for how the organization addresses its actual and potential negative impacts Source: Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD), OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises , 2011; modified United Nations (UN), Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights: Implementing the United Nations “Protect, Respect and Remedy” Framework , 2011; modified Note: See section 2.3 in GRI 1: Foundation 2021 for more information on ‘due diligence’. employee individual who is in an employment relationship with the organization according to national law GRI 1: Foundation 2021 27",
    "new_id": 922,
    "attempt": "A",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific nuances of GRI 1: Foundation 2021 regarding the term business partner. I must first clarify what is being asked. The question seeks a definition that is both a necessary condition for partnership and compliant with the standard's specific exclusions and definitions. A correct solution must distinguish between the types of entities that are included and those that are explicitly ruled out, such as community groups or controlled entities. I need to identify the relationship between the organization and the partner, the nature of their engagement, and the structural boundaries that define them as separate actors.\n\nLet's start with a broad hypothesis. Perhaps a business partner is any entity that interacts with the company in a commercial capacity. This would include suppliers, contractors, and even local vendors. However, if I test this against the requirement for a formal engagement, I run into a problem. A casual vendor at a trade show might meet a business objective, but they do not have a formal engagement. Therefore, a formal relationship is a necessary component. But is formal engagement alone sufficient? If a parent company engages with its subsidiary, they have a formal relationship, but is the subsidiary a \"business partner\" or just a controlled entity? This suggests that the definition must also account for the control relationship.\n\nLet's pivot. What if the definition focuses on the value chain? If an entity is part of the value chain, it is a partner. But GRI 1 often distinguishes between the entity's own operations and its broader ecosystem. If I define a partner as anyone in the value chain, I might include entities that are actually part of the reporting organization's own structure. This leads me to a conflict. If a company owns a subsidiary, that subsidiary is part of the group, not a \"business partner\" in the sense of an external stakeholder. So, the definition must exclude entities that are under the organization's control.\n\nWait, I need to be careful about the distinction between \"engagement\" and \"control.\" If the definition requires a formal engagement, does that automatically exclude controlled entities? Not necessarily. A parent company can have a formal contract with a subsidiary. To satisfy the GRI requirement, the definition must explicitly state that even if there is a formal engagement, if the entity is controlled by the organization, it does not count as a business partner. This creates a tension between the procedure of engagement and the structure of ownership.\n\nLet's look at the community aspect. Option D suggests that community support entities could be partners. If I test this, a community group might work with a company to support local infrastructure, but that group is not a \"business partner\" in the traditional sense of a commercial relationship. GRI 1 usually treats community groups as a separate category of stakeholders. If I include them in the definition of a business partner, I am blurring the line between stakeholder types, which would likely violate the standard's precision. Therefore, the definition must limit business partners to those engaged in commercial or formal business-related objectives, excluding purely social or community-based actors.\n\nNow I have two competing paths. One path defines a partner by what they do: they engage in the value chain and meet business objectives. The other path defines them by what they are: they are external entities with whom the organization has a formal relationship. If I combine these, I get a more robust hypothesis. A business partner must be an external entity that is not controlled by the organization, is engaged in a formal relationship, and is part of the value chain or involved in business-related activities.\n\nLet's stress-test this combined direction. If the entity is a supplier in a secondary tier, they meet the value chain criterion and are external. If they have a contract, they meet the formal engagement criterion. If they are not owned by the company, they meet the control exclusion. This seems to cover the necessary conditions. I must ensure I don't overreach. Does the definition need to specify \"directly linked to the value chain\"? If I say \"any entity in the value chain,\" I might be too broad. But if I say \"directly linked,\" does that exclude the broader ecosystem? The term \"beyond the first tier\" might be necessary to capture the full scope of the ecosystem while maintaining the link to the organization's objectives.\n\nThe strongest direction must integrate the formal relationship, the exclusion of controlled entities, and the distinction from purely community-based actors. The most difficult part is the interplay between the requirement for a formal engagement and the specific exclusion of entities under the organization's control. A correct solution must ensure these two requirements do not contradict each other; rather, they must function as complementary filters. One filter looks at the nature of the relationship (formal and external), while the other looks at the structural independence of the entity (not controlled by the organization). I need to focus on how these two constraints work together to define the boundary of the term.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
