    "ref_doc": "SASB Air Freight & Logistics.pdf",
    "source_text": "5In disclosing fuel consumed by (2) air transport-related operations, the entity shall additionally disclose the percentage of fuel consumed that was (b) sustainable fuel. 5.1 Sustainable fuel is defined as a subset of alternative fuel that meets all of the following criteria described by ICAO: 5.1.1 Achieves net greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions reduction on a life cycle basis 5.1.2 Avoids competition with food and water through utilisation of marginal or unviable land 5.1.3 Contributes to local social and economic development, such as through expanded employment and revitalised infrastructure. 5.2 The percentage shall be calculated as the amount of sustainable fuel consumed by air transport-related operations (in GJ) divided by the total amount of fuel consumed by air transport-related operations (in GJ). 6 The scope of disclosure is limited to fuel the entity directly consumed. 7In calculating energy consumption from fuels, the entity shall use higher heating values (HHV), also known as gross calorific values (GCV), which are measured directly or taken from the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC). 8The entity shall apply conversion factors consistently for all data reported under this disclosure, such as the use of HHVs for fuel use (including biofuels). SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |AIR FREIGHT & LOGISTICS |12\n\n[Page 13]\nAir Quality Topic Summary Entities in the Air Freight & Logistics industry generate air pollutants that may threaten human health. The industry ’s primary air emissions include sulphur oxides (SOx), nitrogen oxides (NOx) and particulate matter (PM), which negatively affect local air quality. As regulators debate the most efficient mechanisms to reduce local air pollution from the industry, entities may be forced to increase operating costs or make investments to modernise their fleets because of regulatory pressure, customer demand and rising fuel costs. Use of more expensive alternative fuels and mechanisms that filter emissions prior to release into the atmosphere also may affect an entity ’s cost structure, requiring upfront costs but decreasing regulatory exposure over the long term. Metrics TR-AF-120a.1. Air emissions of the following pollutants: (1) NO x (excluding N 2O), (2) SO x, and (3) particulate matter (PM 10) 1The entity shall disclose its emissions of air pollutants, in metric tonnes per pollutant, released into the atmosphere. 1.1 The scope of the disclosure includes air pollutants associated with the entity ’s direct air emissions resulting from all the entity ’s activities and sources of emissions, which may include stationary or mobile sources, production facilities, office buildings and transportation fleets. 2 The entity shall disclose its emissions of (1) oxides of nitrogen (NO x), reported as NO x. 2.1 The scope of NO x includes NO and NO 2, but excludes N 2O. 3 The entity shall disclose its emissions of (2) oxides of sulphur (SO x), reported as SO x. 3.1 The scope of SO x includes SO 2 and SO 3. 4The entity shall disclose its emissions of (3) particulate matter 10 micrometres or less in diameter (PM 10), reported as PM 10. 4.1 PM10 is defined as any airborne finely divided solid or liquid material with an aerodynamic diameter less than or equal to a nominal 10 micrometres. 5The entity may discuss the calculation method for its emissions disclosure, such as whether data is from continuous emissions monitoring systems (CEMS), engineering calculations or mass balance calculations. SUSTAINABILITY ACCOUNTING STANDARD |AIR FREIGHT & LOGISTICS |13",
    "new_id": 998,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in interpreting the specific disclosure requirements for sustainable fuel within the Air Freight & Logistics sector. I must first clarify what is meant by a necessary condition. In logic, a necessary condition is one that must be present for the statement to be true, but it does not alone prove the statement. This is distinct from a sufficient condition, which would prove the statement on its own. The term sustainable is highly ambiguous here; it could refer to a broad ESG profile or a specific technical metric. To find the correct answer, I must identify which set of constraints the standard uses to define the boundary of what qualifies as sustainable fuel for reporting purposes.\n\nLet's test Option A. It states the fuel must achieve net greenhouse gas emissions reduction on a life cycle basis and be derived exclusively from non-food sources. This is a strong technical hypothesis. Reducing net GHG is a standard metric for climate mitigation, and excluding food sources is a common mechanism to prevent land-use conflicts that lead to indirect land-use change. However, is exclusivity from food sources a necessary condition for all sustainable fuel, or is it a specific requirement for certain types of biofuels? If the standard allows for sustainable fuels that are food-based but meet other rigorous criteria, then this is not a universal necessary condition. I will reject Option A because it is too narrow; it focuses on the feedstock rather than the holistic requirements of the disclosure.\n\nMoving to Option B, the focus shifts to the social and economic dimensions of fuel sourcing. It requires the fuel to avoid competition with food and water and to contribute to local development. This sounds like a social sustainability approach. But does a fuel \"contribute to local development\" as a necessary condition for being classified as sustainable? This seems more like an output of a responsible supply chain than a fundamental chemical or carbon requirement for the fuel itself. If the standard only cares about carbon footprints, this is an incorrect expansion. However, if the standard integrates ESG, then this social dimension must be part of the definition. I need to see if this social component is tied to a specific technical threshold.\n\nLet's look at the relationship between these ideas. If I combine the technical GHG reduction from Option A with the social requirements from Option B, I get a more complex set of criteria. But wait, there is a third dimension mentioned in the problem: the distinction between direct and indirect operations. If a fuel meets all these criteria—GHG reduction, no food/water competition, and social contribution—but is only used in operations outside the entity's direct control, does that make it sustainable for the purpose of the entity's reporting? This is a crucial distinction. If the standard only mandates these criteria for third-party or indirect operations, then those specific conditions are the necessary triggers for disclosure.\n\nI should test a counterexample. Suppose a fuel meets all three criteria (GHG, food/water, social) but is used in the entity's own aircraft. Would it still be classified as sustainable under this standard? If the standard's logic is that these specific criteria are only the benchmark for \"sustainable\" when applied to indirect operations, then the fuel would not be classified as such in the entity's direct operations. This suggests that the \"necessary condition\" is not just the fuel's properties, but the specific combination of those properties plus the operational context.\n\nLet's re-examine Option C. It captures the triad of GHG reduction, food/water avoidance, and social contribution, but it specifically links this triad to operations outside the entity's direct control. This introduces a causal link: the presence of these three factors is the necessary condition, but only when the mechanism of consumption is indirect. This is a much more sophisticated hypothesis. It distinguishes between the attributes of the fuel and the scope of the reporting entity. \n\nI must be careful not to confuse the mechanism with the actor. The actor is the logistics company; the mechanism is the fuel's lifecycle; the output is the reduction in emissions; and the constraint is the avoidance of food and water competition. If the standard requires that the fuel must also promote local development, I have to decide if that is a hard technical requirement or a qualitative one. If the standard is purely quantitative, the social aspect is irrelevant. But in an ESG context, the \"S\" (Social) is a pillar. If the standard integrates ESG, the social contribution is a necessary component of the \"sustainable\" label.\n\nIs there any other possibility? Option D introduces sulphur oxide emissions. While reducing sulphur is good for the environment, it is not a standard component of the ESG definitions of sustainable fuel that I am familiar with in the context of biofuels and alternative jet fuels. The focus in these standards is almost always on the carbon lifecycle and land-use impacts. Therefore, I will reject Option D as it introduces a tangential environmental metric that doesn't align with the core ESG framework of the problem.\n\nThis leaves me with a choice between the technical-only path and the integrated ESG path. The technical-only path (GHG reduction and feedstock type) is too narrow. The social-only path (food/water and development) is too broad and qualitative. The strongest direction is the integrated path that links specific technical and social criteria to a specific scope of operations. The necessary condition is not just the fuel's properties, but the specific set of properties required by the standard, specifically when applied to the consumption of third parties or indirect operations. This distinguishes the fuel's performance from the reporting entity's direct responsibility.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
