    "correct_answer": "B",
    "ref_page": "9-10",
    "ref_doc": "IFRS S2 Vol65.pdf",
    "source_text": "the Green-e Framework for Renewable Energy Certification, Version 1.0 (2017) or Green-e regional standards, or materials that are eligible for an applicable jurisdictional renewable portfolio standard. 5 The entity shall apply conversion factors consistently for all data reported under this disclosure, such as the use of HHVs for fuel use (including biofuels) and conversion of kilowatt hours (kWh) to GJ (for energy data including electricity from solar or wind energy). TR-CL-110a.4. Average Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI) for new ships 1 The entity shall disclose the average Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI) for new ships in grammes of carbon dioxide per ton-nautical mile. 1.1 An EEDI value is the product of power installed, specific fuel consumption and carbon conversion, divided by the product of available capacity and vessel speed at design load 1.2 The entity shall calculate the average EEDI as a simple average of the EEDI value of all new ships added to the entity ’s fleet during the reporting period. 1.2.1 New ships are limited to those built after 2013 and for which the International Maritime Organisation (IMO) has adopted EEDI as a metric. 1.3 The entity shall follow calculation methodologies outlined in IMO MEPC 66/21/Add.1, Annex 5, 2014 Guidelines on the Method of Calculation of the Attained Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI) For New Ships .IFRS S2 INDUSTRY-BASED GUIDANCE 8 © IFRS Foundation\n\n[Page 10]\n© IFRS Foundation 9 Columbus Building 7 Westferry Circus Canary Wharf London E14 4HD, UK Tel +44 (0) 20 7246 6410 Email sustainability_licensing@ifrs.org ifrs.org International Financial Reporting Standards®, IFRS Foundation®, IFRS®, IAS®, IFRIC®, SIC®, IASB®, ISSBTM, IFRS for SMEs®",
    "new_id": 1106,
    "attempt": "B",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying the precise mathematical and temporal mechanism used to derive the average Energy Efficiency Design Index for a fleet of cruise ships. I must first clarify the ambiguity in the term components. Does this refer to the technical variables used to calculate the EEDI of a single vessel, or does it refer to the individual EEDI values of the vessels that comprise the fleet average? Furthermore, the term underlying components could imply a hierarchy of data, such as fuel types or speed ratings, that must be accounted for in the aggregate calculation. To solve this, a correct response must accurately link the selection of vessels to the method of averaging their indices.\n\nLet us test the hypothesis that the components are the technical variables themselves. If the components are power installed, specific fuel consumption, and carbon conversion, then the calculation of the average EEDI would involve summing these variables across all ships and then dividing. However, this approach is flawed because it ignores the fundamental nature of the EEDI as a ratio-based index. Averaging the raw inputs of a ratio does not yield the same result as averaging the ratios themselves. Therefore, the components must be the individual EEDI values of the vessels.\n\nThis leads to a second path: investigating the weighting mechanism. If the average is a weighted average, what is the weight? Is it displacement, speed, or construction date? If the weight were speed, the average would reflect a bias toward faster vessels. If the weight were displacement, it would favor larger ships. However, the problem asks for the relationship between the average and its components, and a simple mean is a much more straightforward relationship than a weighted one. If the components are the individual EEDI values and the average is a simple mean, the relationship is direct and additive.\n\nI must now consider the temporal constraint mentioned in the prompt. The guidance specifies a cutoff for new ships. If I ignore this, I might include legacy vessels that do not meet modern EEDI standards, which would skew the average. The inclusion rule must be a specific threshold, likely a year, to distinguish between the old and new regimes. Testing the year 2013 is logical because that is the year the initial EEDI requirements came into effect for certain ship types. This means the components of the average are not just any EEDI values, but specifically those of ships built after 2013.\n\nI should also evaluate whether the components include fuel-specific conversion factors. While it is true that biofuils require higher heating value (HHV) adjustments for accurate carbon accounting, the question asks about the relationship between the average EEDI and its components. The HHV is a conversion factor applied to the energy data, not a component of the EEDI index itself. Including it might be a distraction regarding the specific mathematical relationship of the index values.\n\nSimilarly, I must reject the idea that Green-e regional standards or renewable portfolio standards are part of the EEDI calculation. These are certification and regulatory frameworks for electricity, whereas EEDI is a measure of a ship's design efficiency in terms of fuel consumption per unit of transport work. Mixing these would be a category error in the mechanism of the index.\n\nThe strongest direction for the solution is to synthesize the selection criteria and the aggregation method. The components are the EEDI values of the vessels, the selection is restricted to those built after 2013, and the relationship is a simple average of these selected values. This avoids the error of using weighted averages or incorrect temporal boundaries and focuses on the direct mathematical link between the parts and the whole.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
