    "correct_answer": "C",
    "ref_page": "3-4",
    "ref_doc": "IFRS S2 Vol65.pdf",
    "source_text": "Introduction This volume is part of the Industry-based Guidance on Implementing IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures . This guidance suggests possible ways to apply some of the disclosure requirements in IFRS S2 but does not create additional requirements. This volume suggests possible ways to identify, measure and disclose information about climate-related risks and opportunities that are associated with particular business models, economic activities and other common features that characterise participation in this industry. This industry-based guidance has been derived from Sustainability Accounting Standards Board (SASB) Standards, which are maintained by the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB). The metric codes used in SASB Standards have been included for ease of reference. For additional context regarding the industry-based guidance contained in this volume, including structure and terminology, application and illustrative examples, refer to Section III of the Accompanying Guidance to IFRS S2.IFRS S2 INDUSTRY-BASED GUIDANCE 2 © IFRS Foundation\n\n[Page 4]\nVolume 65 —Cruise Lines Industry Description Cruise Lines industry entities provide passenger transportation and leisure entertainment, including deep sea cruises and river cruises. A few large entities dominate the industry. Cruises provide a luxury resort experience for thousands of passengers at a time. The Cruise Lines industry often has been the fastest-growing segment of the travel industry, but it is very cyclical. Sustainability Disclosure Topics & Metrics Table 1. Sustainability Disclosure Topics & Metrics TOPIC METRIC CATEGORY UNIT OF MEASURECODE Greenhouse Gas EmissionsGross global Scope 1 emissions Quantitative Metric tons (t) CO₂-eTR-CL-110a.1 Discussion of long- and short-term strategy or plan to manage Scope 1 emissions, emissions reduction targets, and an analysis of performance against those targetsDiscussion and Analysisn/a TR-CL-110a.2 (1) Total energy consumed, (2) percentage heavy fuel oil, (3) percentage onshore power supply (OPS) and (4) percentage renewableQuantitative Gigajoules (GJ), Percentage (%)TR-CL-110a.3 Average Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI) for new shipsQuantitative Grammes of CO₂ per ton- nautical mileTR-CL-110a.4 Table 2. Activity Metrics ACTIVITY METRIC CATEGORY UNIT OF MEASURECODE Available lower berth kilometres (ALB-KM) 117Quantitative ALB-KM TR-CL-000.A Average passenger cruise days (APCD) 118Quantitative APCD TR-CL-000.B Number of shipboard employees 119Quantitative Number TR-CL-000.C Cruise passengers 120Quantitative Number TR-CL-000.D Number of vessel port calls Quantitative Number TR-CL-000.E 117Note to TR-CL-000.A – Available lower berth (ALB) is a measure of the standard capacity of a cruise ship, usually assuming two people per available cabin. It accounts for changes in fleet size, itineraries, and passenger capacity. Available lower berth kilometres (ALB-KM) are computed by multiplying ALB on each leg by the number of kilometres travelled on that leg. 118Note to TR-CL-000.B – Average passenger cruise days (APCD) is computed as the number of available lower berths on a ship multiplied by the number of days that those berths are available to passengers during the reporting period. 119Note to TR-CL-000.C – Shipboard employees are those employees who work aboard the entity ’s vessels (including direct and contract employees) during the reporting period. 120Note to TR-CL-000.D – Cruise passengers is the number of passengers aboard the entity ’s vessels, excluding employees.IFRS S2 CLIMATE-RELATED DISCLOSURES –JUNE 2023 © IFRS Foundation 3",
    "new_id": 1107,
    "attempt": "C",
    "reasoning": "\nThe core of this problem lies in identifying the precise relationship between the aggregate metric, the Average Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI), and its constituent parts. I must first clarify what is meant by the relationship. Is it a mathematical aggregation, a causal dependency, or a qualitative categorization? The term components is the critical variable; it implies that the EEDI is not a single, monolithic measurement but a composite of various data points. A correct solution must explain how these parts interact to form the whole, specifically within the context of the IFRS S2 guidance for cruise lines. I need to determine if the relationship is defined by the data's temporal nature, its operational scope, its capacity metrics, or its demographic segmentation.\n\nLet's test the first hypothesis: the relationship is defined by the temporal direction of the data. If the EEDI were a retrospective indicator, it would rely on historical emissions data to calculate past performance. However, the EEDI is fundamentally a design-based index, which means it is intended to reflect the technical efficiency of the fleet's design to meet future regulatory standards. Therefore, Option A is incorrect because it misidentifies the EEDI as a historical rather than a design-based metric.\n\nMoving to a second hypothesis: the relationship is defined by the operational scope of the components. If I look at the inclusion of the onshore power supply (OPS) percentage in TR-CL-110a.3, I see a mechanism that addresses a specific operational variable. The OPS percentage acts as a modifier or a specific component within the broader calculation. This suggests that the EEDI is not a single number but a composite that includes variables like OPS to account for the energy source during port calls. This provides a more granular way to understand the fleet's efficiency. This path seems strong because it shows how a specific component directly influences the aggregate index.\n\nI should consider a third path: the relationship is defined by capacity metrics. The mention of available lower berth kilometres (ALB-KM) introduces a new dimension. If I treat ALB-KM as a simple capacity metric, I might conclude it is just a measure of how many passengers the ship can hold. But the problem asks for its role in the relationship with the EEDI. If the EEDI is a measure of energy efficiency per unit of distance, then a capacity metric like ALB-KM could serve as the denominator or a normalization factor. This would mean the EEDI is not just a measure of energy used, but a measure of energy used per unit of capacity-distance. This would make the EEDI a measure of intensity rather than just an absolute volume. This is a more sophisticated way to view the relationship, but I must be careful. Does the guidance explicitly link ALB-KM to the EEDI, or are they just reported side-by-side? If they are side-by-side, then ALB-KM is a component of fleet data, but not a direct mathematical component of the EEDI formula. If they are linked, the relationship is one of normalization.\n\nLet's test a fourth, perhaps more complex, hypothesis: the relationship is defined by demographic segmentation. The mention of tracking cruise passengers and shipboard employees separately suggests a way to manage different types of human impact. If the EEDI were related to these groups, it might imply a way to separate the carbon footprint of the operations (the employees) from the carbon footprint of the customers (the passengers). However, the EEDI is a technical index for energy efficiency, not a social or human impact index. The guidance might require this segmentation for other sustainability disclosures, but it does not change the technical nature of the EEDI itself. Therefore, the relationship between the EEDI and these demographic groups is likely indirect or non-existent in terms of the technical calculation.\n\nI need to weigh the strength of the operational scope hypothesis against the capacity metric hypothesis. The operational scope hypothesis is more direct; it identifies how a specific variable like OPS is integrated into the index to reflect real-world operational choices. The capacity metric hypothesis is more subtle; it suggests that the EEDI is part of a larger data ecosystem where capacity metrics are used to provide context or normalize the efficiency data. \n\nIs there a flaw in assuming the EEDI is a simple average? If the EEDI were just a simple average of all ships, it would be a straightforward arithmetic relationship. But if the guidance specifies how certain percentages or specific operational modes are factored in, then the relationship is more complex, involving weighted or conditional components. The strongest direction for a solution must focus on how the EEDI functions as a composite index that incorporates specific operational variables, such as the OPS percentage, to reflect the fleet's technical efficiency. The relationship is one where the aggregate index is built from specific, context-dependent components that allow for the measurement of design-based energy performance.\n**Final Answer**"
  },
